#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:spriggan baker"
msgid "“Blood, sweat, and tea, sister! That's what it takes to achieve all great and\n terrible things.”\n    -Emory R. Frie, _Wonderland_. 2016.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:witch"
msgid "“Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live.”\n    -KJV Bible, Exodus 22:18.\n\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__c_suffix"
msgid "“When Peleus, some distance away, saw him torn apart by the frightful wound he\n shouted: ‘Accept this tribute to the dead, at least, Crantor, dearest of\n youths’, and with his powerful arm, he hurled his ash spear, at full strength,\n at Demoleon. It ruptured the ribcage, and stuck quivering in the bone. The\n centaur pulled out the shaft minus its head (he tried with difficulty to reach\n that also) but the head was caught in his lung. The pain itself strengthened\n his will: wounded, he reared up at his enemy and beat the hero down with his\n hooves. Peleus received the resounding blows on helmet and shield, and\n defending his upper arms, and controlling the weapon he held out, with one\n blow through the arm he pierced the bi-formed breast.”\n    -Ovid, _Metamorphoses_, XII, 330. 8 AD.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__d_suffix"
msgid "“Trogdor was a man!\n I mean, he was a dragon-man!\n Or... maybe he was just a dragon.\n ...\n And the Trogdor comes in the night!”\n    -The Brothers Chaps, “Trogdor”. 2003.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__q_suffix"
msgid "<__d_suffix>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__r_suffix"
msgid "HAMLET [Drawing his sword.]: How now! a rat? Dead, for a ducat, dead\n       [Stabs through the arras.]\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark_, III, 4.\n1603.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__cap-d_suffix"
msgid "“On the other hand, Confucius is made to say to his disciples, ‘I know how\n birds can fly, how fishes can swim, and how animals can run. But the runner\n may be snared, the swimmer may be hooked, and the flyer may be shot by the\n arrow. But there is the dragon. I cannot tell how he mounts on the wind\n through the clouds, and rises to heaven. Today I have seen Lao-tsze, and can\n only compare him to the dragon.’”\n    -Life of Confucius\n\n“This Dragon had Two furious Wings\n   Each one upon each Shoulder;\n With a Sting in his Tail as long as a Flail,\n   Which made him bolder and bolder.\n He had long Claws, and in his Jaws\n   Four and forty Teeth of Iron;\n With a Hide as tough, as any Buff,\n   Which did him round environ.”\n\n    -“An Excellent Ballad of a most dreadful Combat, fought between Moore of\nMoore-Hall, and the Dragon of Wantley”, retold by Ambrose Philips, _A\nCollection of Old Ballads. Corrected from the Best and Most Ancient Copies\nExtant. With Introductions Historical, Critical, Or Humorous_. 1723.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__cap-k_suffix"
msgid "“The Parts Septentrionall are with these Sp'ryts Much haunted.. About the\n places where they dig for Oare. The Greekes and Germans call them Cobali.”\n    -Thomas Heywood, _The Hierarchy of the Blessed Angels_, Book IX, l. 568.\n1635.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__cap-s_suffix"
msgid "“The latter lived in the country, and before his house there was an oak, in\n which there was a lair of snakes. His servants killed the snakes, but Melampus\n gathered wood and burnt the reptiles, and reared the young ones. And when the\n young were full grown, they stood beside him at each of his shoulders as he\n slept, and they purged his ears with their tongues. He started up in a great\n fright, but understood the voices of the birds flying overhead, and from what\n he learned from them he foretold to men what should come to pass.”\n    -Pseudo-Apollodorus , _Library and Epitome_, 1.9.11. circa 150 BC.\n    trans. Sir James George Frazer, 1913.\n\n“A snake, with mottles rare,\n Surveyed my chamber floor,\n In feature as the worm before,\n But ringed with power.”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “In Winter In My Room”. circa 1860.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:__cap-t_suffix"
msgid "“Buckshank bold and Elfinstone,\n And more than I can mention here,\n They caused to be built so stout a ship,\n And unto Iceland they would steer.\n\n They launched the ship upon the main,\n Which bellowed like a wrathful bear;\n Down to the bottom the vessel sank,\n A laidly Trold has dragged it there.”\n    -George Borrow, _Lavengro: The Scholar, the Gypsy, the Priest_. 1851.\n\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:adder"
msgid "ADDER, n. A species of snake. So called from its habit of adding funeral outlays\nto the other expenses of living.\n    -Ambrose Bierce, _The Devil's Dictionary_, 1906.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:alderking"
msgid "““My father, my father, he seizes me fast,\n Full sorely the Erl-King has hurt me at last.”\n\n The father now gallops, with terror half wild,\n He grasps in his arms the poor shuddering child;\n He reaches his courtyard with toil and with dread,\n The child in his arms finds he motionless, dead.”\n    -Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, _Erlkönig_, 1782.\n    trans. Edgar Alfred Bowring, 1853.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:alligator"
msgid "“Alligators commit errors of diet.”\n    -Bennet Bowler, M.D., _Contributions to the Natural History of the\n     Alligator, (Crocodilus Mississipiensis), with a Microscopic Addendum_,\n     p. 17. 1846.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:apocalypse crab"
msgid "“I should have been a pair of ragged claws\n scuttling across the floors of silent seas.”\n    -T.S. Eliot, _The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock_. 1915.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:arcanist"
msgid "<occultist>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:basilisk"
msgid "“Be thou like the imperial Basilisk\n Killing thy foe with unapparent wounds!\n Gaze on Oppression, till at that dread risk\n Aghast she pass from the Earth's disk:\n Fear not, but gaze—for freemen mightier grow,\n And slaves more feeble, gazing on their foe:—\n If Hope, and Truth, and Justice may avail,\n Thou shalt be great—All hail!”\n    -Percy Bysshe Shelley, “Posthumous Poems”. 1824.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:bat"
msgid "“The sun was set; the night came on apace,\n And falling dews bewet around the place;\n The bat takes airy rounds on leathern wings,\n And the hoarse owl his woeful dirges sings.”\n    -John Gay, Shepherd's Week, Wednesday; or, The Dumps.\n\n“Ere the bat hath flown\n His cloister'd flight.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _Macbeth_, III, 2, line 40. 1605.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:battlesphere"
msgid "“I'm your only friend\n I'm not your only friend\n But I'm a little glowing friend\n But really I'm not actually your friend\n But I am”\n    -They Might Be Giants, “Birdhouse in Your Soul”. 1989.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:bes kemwar"
msgid "“The Bès Kěmwar, “maggot spirit”, is one of the many _bès_ or disease spirits\n known to the Jah Hut of Malaysia. A polyvalent spirit, it is depicted as a\n maggot or caterpillar, with variants distinguished by hair, thorns, wings, and\n other details. It eats rice, vegetables, and other crops. It is responsible for\n aches in bones, joints, and muscles. If its caterpillar hair falls into water\n and that water is drunk, it causes coughing and bleeding in the throat. Maggot\n spirits are also known to live in rotten tree trunks and feed in them. Anyone\n who approaches the fallen tree will be bitten in the leg by the spirit, causing\n redness, swelling, and itching. All the toenails fall off but the swelling will\n be gone by two or three months.”\n    - _A Book of Creatures_, 2020.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:boggart"
msgid "“He thinks every bush a boggart.”\n    -John Ray, _A Compleat Collection of English Proverbs_. 1768.\n\n“A BOGGART intruded himself, upon what pretext or by what authority is unknown,\n into the house of a quiet, inoffensive, and laborious farmer; and, when once\n it had taken possession it disputed the right of domicile with the legal\n mortal tenant, in a very unneighbourly and arbitrary manner. In particular, it\n seemed to have a great aversion to children. As there is no point on which a\n parent feels more acutely than that of the maltreatment of his offspring, the\n feelings of the father and more particularly of his good dame, were daily, ay,\n and nightly, harrowed up by the malice of this malignant and invisible\n boggart.”\n    -C.J.T., _Folk-lore and Legends: English_  1890.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:bullfrog"
msgid "“Hello, my baby\n Hello, my honey\n Hello, my ragtime gal\n\n Send me a kiss by wire\n Baby, my hearts on fire\n\n If you refuse me\n Honey, you'll lose me\n Then you'll be left alone\n\n Oh baby, telephone\n And tell me I'm your own.”\n    -Ida Emerson and Joseph E. Howard, “Hello My Baby!”\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:bush"
msgid "“And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him in a flame of fire out of the\n midst of a bush: and he looked, and, behold, the bush burned with fire, and\n the bush was not consumed.”\n    -KJV Bible, Exodus 3:2.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:butterfly"
msgid "“Happiness is a butterfly, which when pursued, is always just beyond your\n grasp, but which, if you will sit down quietly, may alight upon you.”\n    -Nathaniel Hawthorne\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:cacodemon"
msgid "“We'll call him Cacodemon, with his black Gib there, his Succuba, his Devil's\n Seed, his Spawn of Phlegethon, that o’ my Consience was bred o’ the Spume of\n Cocytus.”\n    -John Fletcher, _The Knight of Malta_. 1647.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:catoblepas"
msgid "“So passed he over into the island, taking with him the two brothers of\n Anaxius; where he found the forsaken knight attired in his own livery, as\n black as sorrow itself could see itself in the blackest glass: his ornaments\n of the same hue, but formd into the figures of ravens which seemed to gape for\n carrion: only his reins were snakes, which finely wrapping themselves one\n within the other, their heads came together to the cheeks and bosses of the\n bit, where they might seem to bite at the horse, and the horse, as he champed\n the bit, to bite at them, and that the white foam was engendered by the\n poisonous fury of the combat. His impresa was a Catoblepta, which so long lies\n dead as the moon (whereto it hath so natural a sympathy) wants her light. The\n word signified, that the moon wanted not the light, but the poor beast wanted\n the moon's light.”\n    -Sir Philip Sidney\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:cherub"
msgid "“The glory of Yahweh mounted up from the cherub, and stood over the threshold\n of the house; and the house was filled with the cloud, and the court was full\n of the brightness of Yahweh's glory.\n\n The sound of the wings of the cherubim was heard even to the outer court, as\n the voice of God Almighty when he speaks.”\n    -WEB Bible, Ezekiel 10:4-5\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:chonchon"
msgid "“In its simplest form it is a bird of the night, an owl (_Strix rufipes_) that\n flies on silent wings during the night to announce illness, death, or some\n other unwelcome event. When its croaking is heard it is advised to throw ash\n into the air and utter a few prayers in the hopes of turning away its evil.\n\n The chonchón is also said to be a _calcu_ or evil sorcerer in disguise. It\n resemble a human head, with oversized wing-like ears that allow it to fly.\n During the night, these sorcerers’ heads detach themselves from their bodies\n and fly around to cause mischief, invisible to most, their ominous _tué, tué,\n tué_ call announcing misfortune. They have the same powers as sorcerers do,\n and have been known to suck the blood of sleepers.”\n    - _A Book of Creatures_, 2015.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:crimson imp"
msgid "“The Devil, too, sometimes steals human children; it is not infrequent for him\n to carry away infants within the first six weeks after birth, and to\n substitute in their place imps.”\n    -Martin Luther\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:crocodile"
msgid "“How doth the little crocodile\n  Improve his shining tail,\n And pour the waters of the Nile\n  On every golden scale!\n\n How cheerfully he seems to grin,\n  How neatly spreads his claws,\n And welcomes little fishes in\n  With gently smiling jaws!”\n    -Lewis Carroll, _Alice's Adventures in Wonderland_, 1865.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:curse skull"
msgid "“Good friend for Jesus’ sake forbear,\n To dig the dust enclosed here.\n Blessed be the man that spares these stones,\n And cursed be he that moves my bones.”\n    -Epitaph on William Shakespeare's grave\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:daeva"
msgid "“Between these twain the Daevas also chose not aright, for infatuation came\n upon them as they took counsel together, so that they chose the Worst Thought.\n Then they rushed together to Violence, that they might enfeeble the world of\n men.”\n    -the Avesta, Yasna XXX, 6, Ahunavaiti Gatha.\n    trans. Christian Bartholomae, 1951.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:demonspawn soul scholar"
msgid "“Xinchub: At least you're conceding that I've got a soul.\n   Petey: Yes, but it is battered, twisted, and drenched in the blood of the\n          innocent.\n Xinchub: Mmm... yeah.\n   Petey: When you stop being pleased by that we'll know you're making\n          progress.”\n    -Howard Tayler, _Schlock Mercenary_. February 8, 2008.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:diamond obelisk"
msgid "“As one example of the meanings inherent in a form, let us examine a particular\n form of vertical stone marker, variously called stele, obelisk, standing-stone,\n and memorial column. These have been historically and commonly used to\n commemorate honored phenomena. So, when a people wish to remember an important\n relationship, event or personage, a location is dedicated to it and often\n marked with an enduring and aspiring vertical form or sets of them. In natural\n language, a vertical stone means: an aspiring connector between us (on earth)\n and an ideal (up there); that we ‘stand up’ with pride about this honored\n phenomenon. The marker is a symbolic inhabitation of the place it occupies. Its\n size and workmanship is a sacrifice of much work and resources to a memory. It\n is a strong suggestion (because we left it to them and it is of durable\n material) that future people also give honor to the memorialized phenomenon.\n When we use this particular physical form of the vertical marker, both its\n historic use as an honorific and its meanings in natural language may well send\n a message that this is an honored place, a place about a ‘good’ both in our\n culture and in the culture of future observers.”\n    -“Expert Judgment on Markers to Deter Inadvertent Human Intrusion into the\n      Waste Isolation Pilot Plant”, Sandia National Laboratories report. 1993\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:oblivion hound"
msgid "“Standing over Hugo, and plucking at his throat, there stood a foul thing, a\n great, black beast, shaped like a hound, yet larger than any hound that ever\n mortal eye has rested upon. And even as they looked the thing tore the throat\n out of Hugo Baskerville...”\n    -Arthur Conan Doyle, _The Hound of the Baskervilles_. 1902.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:dream sheep"
msgid "“How undisturbed, the sleep of the foolish.”\n    -Philip K. Dick\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:dryad"
msgid " • Roy Quixote: Wait, what's my beef with clean energy again?\n — Durkon Pansa: Dunno, but if'n ye prefer, I know a grove o'peach trees tha've\n   been gettin' fresh wit tha locals.\n    -Rich Burlew, “Haleo and Julean”\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:efreet"
msgid "“When the hoopoe returned to Solomon (he told him the news), and he responded\n (to Sheba's people): “Are you giving me money? What GOD has given me is far\n better than what He has given you. You are the ones to rejoice in such gifts.”\n (To the hoopoe, he said), “Go back to them (and let them know that) we will\n come to them with forces they cannot imagine. We will evict them, humiliated\n and debased.” He said, “O you elders, which of you can bring me her mansion,\n before they arrive here as submitters?” One afrit from the jinns said, “I can\n bring it to you before you stand up. I am powerful enough to do this.”\n    -The Quran, Sura 27 Al-Naml\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:eidolon"
msgid "“No sooner had sleep caught him, dissolving all his grief\n as mists of refreshing slumber poured around him there—\n his powerful frame was bone-weary from charging Hector\n straight and hard to the walls of windswept Troy—\n than the ghost of stricken Patroclus drifted up...\n He was like the man to the life, every feature,\n the same tall build and the fine eyes and voice\n and the very robes that used to clothe his body.\n Hovering at his head the phantom rose and spoke:\n ‘Sleeping, Achilles? You’ve forgotten me, my friend.\n You never neglected me in life, only now in death.\n Bury me, quickly—let me pass the Gates of Hades.\n They hold me off at a distance, all the souls,\n the shades of the burnt-out, breathless dead,\n never to let me cross the river, mingle with them...\n They leave me to wander up and down, abandoned, lost\n at the House of Death with the all-embracing gates.’”\n    -Homer, _The Iliad_, XXIII, 72-88.\n     trans. Robert Fagles, 1990.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:electric golem"
msgid "“I sing the Body electric”\n    -Walt Whitman, “I Sing the Body Electric”, _Leaves of Grass_. 1867.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:elephant"
msgid "“And the King went to where the blind men were, and drawing near said to them:\n ‘Do you now know what an elephant is like?’\n ‘Assuredly, Lord: we now know what an elephant is like.’\n ‘Tell me then, O blind men, what an elephant is like.’\n And those blind men, O Bhikkhus, who had felt the head of the elephant, said:\n ‘An elephant, Sir, is like a large round jar.\n Those who had felt its ears, said: 'it is like a winnowing basket.’\n Those who had felt its tusks, said: ‘it is like a plough-share.’\n Those who had felt its trunk, said: ‘it is like a plough.’\n Those who had felt its body, said: ‘it is like a granary.’\n Those who had felt its feet, said: ‘it is like a pillar.’\n Those who had felt its back, said: ‘it is like a mortar.’\n Those who had felt its tail, said: ‘it is a like a pestle.’\n Those who had felt the tuft of its tail, said: ‘it is like a broom.’\n And they all fought amongst themselves with their fists, declaring, ‘such is\n an elephant, such is not elephant, an elephant is not like that, it is like\n this.’”\n And the King, O Bhikkhus, was highly delighted.\n    -_Udāna_, VI “Jaccandhavagga”. ca. 5th cent. B.C.\n     trans. Dawsonne Melanchthon Strong, 1902.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:emperor scorpion"
msgid "“Portents had occurred indicating [Titus Flavius Vespasianus'] approaching end,\n such as the comet which was visible for a long time and the opening of the\n mausoleum of Augustus of its own accord. When his physicians chided him for\n continuing his usual course of living during his illness and attending to all\n the duties that belonged to his office, he answered: ‘The emperor ought to die\n on his feet.’”\n    -Cassius Dio, _Roman History_, LXVI, xvii, 2. 222 A.D.\n     trans. Earnest Cary, 1925.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:endoplasm"
msgid "“Sea horses floundering in the slimy mud,\n Tossed up their heads, and dashed the ooze about them.”\n    -John Dryden. _All For Love_, I, i, 15-17. 1678.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:entropy weaver"
msgid "“SEE! warp is stretched\n For warrior’s fall;\n Lo, weft in loom,\n ’Tis wet with blood;\n Now, fight foreboding,\n ’Neath friends’ swift fingers\n Our gray woof waxeth\n With war’s alarms,\n Our warp blood-red,\n Our weft corse-blue.\n\n This woof is y-woven\n With entrails of men;\n This warp is hard weighted\n With heads of the slain;\n Spears blood-besprinkled\n For spindles we use,\n Our loom iron-bound,\n And arrows our reels;\n With swords for our shuttles\n This war-woof we work;\n So weave we, weird sisters,\n Our war-winning woof.\n\n Now war-winner walketh\n To weave in her turn,\n Now Sword-swinger steppeth,\n Now Swift-stroke, now Storm;\n When they speed the shuttle\n How spear-heads shall flash!\n Shields crash, and helm-gnawer\n On harness bite hard!”\n    -_The Story of Burnt Njal_, trans. George Dasent. 1861\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ettin"
msgid "“But he had not been long in his hiding-hole, before the awful Ettin came in;\n and no sooner was he in, than he was heard crying:\n ‘Snouk but and snouk ben,\n  I find the smell of an earthly man,\n  Be he living, or be he dead,\n  His heart this night shall kitchen my bread.’”\n    -Joseph Jacobs, _The Red Ettin_\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:executioner"
msgid "“Behold the Lord High Executioner\n A personage of noble rank and title —\n A dignified and potent officer\n Whose functions are particularly vital!\n Defer, defer\n To the Lord High Executioner!\n Defer, defer\n To the noble Lord, to the noble Lord\n To the Lord High Executioner!”\n    -Gilbert and Sullivan, “Behold the Lord High Executioner”,\n      _The Mikado_. 1885\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:fenstrider witch"
msgid "“All the infections that sun sucks up\n From bogs, fens, flats, on Prosper fall and make him\n By inch-meal a disease! His spirits hear me\n And yet I needs must curse.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tempest_, II, 2. 1611.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:fire crab"
msgid "“The planet brought forth scintillating jewelled scuttling crabs, which the\n Vogons ate, smashing their shells with iron mallets; tall aspiring trees with\n breathtaking slenderness and colour which the Vogons cut down and burned the\n crab meat with; elegant gazellelike creatures with silken coats and dewy eyes\n which the Vogons would catch and sit on.”\n    -Douglas Adams, _The Hitch-hiker's Guide to the Galaxy_. 1979.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:flayed ghost"
msgid "“Solamen miseris socios habuisse doloris.”\n    -Christopher Marlowe, _Doctor Faustus_. 1604\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:formless jellyfish"
msgid "“From this story it is evident that in former times the jellyfish once had a\nshell and bones something like a tortoise, but, ever since the Dragon King's\nsentence was carried out on the ancestor of the jelly fishes, his descendants\nhave all been soft and boneless just as you see them to-day thrown up by the\nwaves high upon the shores of Japan.”\n    -“The Jellyfish and the Monkey”, adapted by Yei Theodora Ozaki, _Young\n     Folk's Treasury_, vol. II. 1909.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ghost moth"
msgid "“Always in focus\n You can't feel my stare.\n I zoom into you\n You don't know I'm there.”\n    -Judas Priest, “Electric Eye”. 1982.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ghoul"
msgid "“In the desert\n I saw a creature, naked, bestial,\n Who, squatting upon the ground,\n Held his heart in his hands,\n And ate of it.\n I said, ‘Is it good, friend?’\n ‘It is bitter — bitter’, he answered,\n ‘But I like it\n Because it is bitter,\n And because it is my heart.’”\n    -Stephen Crane, _The Black Riders and Other Lines_. 1895.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:glass eye"
msgid "“El ojo que ves\n no es ojo porque tú lo veas,\n es ojo porque te ve.”\n    -Antonio Machado, Campos de Castilla. 1912-1917.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:gnoll"
msgid "“Then he descended softly and beckoned to Nuth. But the gnoles had watched him\n through knavish holes that they bore in trunks of the trees, and the unearthly\n silence gave way, as it were with a grace, to the rapid screams of Tonker as\n they picked him up from behind — screams that came faster and faster until\n they were incoherent. And where they took him it is not good to ask, and what\n they did with him I shall not say.”\n    -Lord Dunsany, “How Nuth Would Have Practised His Art Upon the Gnoles”.\n1912.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:goblin"
msgid "“Swish, smack! Whip crack!\n Batter and beat! Yammer and bleat!\n Work, work! Nor dare to shirk,\n While Goblins quaff, and Goblins laugh,\n Round and round far underground\n Below, my lad!”\n    -J.R.R. Tolkien, _The Hobbit_\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:golden dragon"
msgid "“You do not come dramatically, with dragons\n That rear up with my life between their paws\n And dash me butchered down beside the wagons”\n    -Philip Larkin, “To Failure”. 1949.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:golden eye"
msgid "“No coveting nor envy burns\n   In thy bright golden eye,\n That calm and innocently turns\n   On all below the sky.”\n    -Hannah Flagg Gould, _The Youth's Coronal_\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:guardian golem"
msgid "“‘Listen Reb _Golem_,’ the old man said, wagging his finger. ‘Pay attention to\n  what I say—you understand?’\n ‘Understand...’\n ‘If you want to stay here, you got to do like Mr. Gumbeiner says.’\n ‘Do-like-Mr.-Gumbeiner-says...’\n ‘_That's_ the way I like to hear a golem talk. Malka, give here the mirror\n  from the pocketbook. Look, you see your face? You see on the forehead, what's\n  written? If you don't do like Mr. Gumbeiner says, he'll wipe out what's\n  written and you'll be no more alive.’\n ‘No-more-alive...’\n ‘_That's_ right. Now, listen. Under the porch you'll find a lawnmower. Take\n  it. And cut the lawn. Then come back. Go.’”\n    -Avram Davidson, “The Golem”, 1955.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:guardian sphinx"
msgid "“The Second Coming! Hardly are those words out\n When a vast image out of Spiritus Mundi\n Troubles my sight: somewhere in sands of the desert\n A shape with lion body and the head of a man,\n A gaze blank and pitiless as the sun,\n Is moving its slow thighs, while all about it\n Reel shadows of the indignant desert birds.”\n    -W. B. Yeats, \"The Second Coming\". 1920.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:harpy"
msgid "“Bird-bodied, girl-faced things they are; abominable their droppings, their\n hands are talons, their faces haggard with hunger insatiable.”\n    -Virgil, Aeneid 3\n\n“And Phineus had scarcely taken the first morsel up when, with as little\n warning as a whirlwind or a lightning flash, they dropped from the clouds\n proclaiming their desire for food with raucous cries. The young lords saw them\n coming and raised the alarm. Yet they had hardly done so before the Harpyiai\n had devoured the whole meal and were on the wing once more, far out at sea.\n All they left was an intolerable stench.”\n    -Apollonius Rhodius, Argonautica 2. 179 — 434\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:hell hound"
msgid "“About her middle round\n A cry of Hell-hounds never-ceasing barked\n With wide Cerberean mouths full loud, and rung\n A hideous peal; yet, when they list, would creep,\n If aught disturbed their noise, into her womb,\n And kennel there; yet there still barked and howled\n Within unseen.”\n    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book II, 1667.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:hell knight"
msgid "“Ok, let's review. It's up to the fair young maiden to rescue the dragon from\n the fire breathing knights in shining armour.”\n    -Exiern\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:hobgoblin"
msgid "“A foolish consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds, adored by little\n statesmen and philosophers and divines.”\n    -Ralph Waldo Emerson, _Essays: First Series_, Essay II: Self-Reliance.\n     1841.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:hog"
msgid "“Fern came slowly down the stairs. Her eyes were red from crying. As she\n approached her chair, the carton wobbled, and there was a scratching noise.\n Fern looked at her father. Then she lifted the lid of the carton. There,\n inside, looking up at her, was the newborn pig. It was a white one. The\n morning light shone through its ears, turning them pink. “He's yours,” said\n Mr. Arable.  “Saved from an untimely death. And may the good Lord forgive me\n for this foolishness.”\n    -E.B. White, _Charlotte's Web_\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:hound"
msgid "“A traveller, by the faithful hound, Half-buried in the snow was found, Still\n grasping in his hand of ice That banner with the strange device, Excelsior!”\n    -Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, “Excelsior”\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:human"
msgid "“Do you know\n Do I know\n What's this thing called ‘man’?\n God only knows what a man is!\n I only know his price.”\n    -Bertolt Brecht, “The Measures Taken”. 1930.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:iguana"
msgid "“Once on a time when Brahmadatta was reigning in Benares, the Bodhisatta was\n born an iguana. When he grew up he dwelt in a big burrow in the river bank\n with a following of many hundreds of other iguanas. Now the Bodhisatta had a\n son, a young iguana, who was great friends with a chameleon, whom he used to\n clip and embrace. This intimacy being reported to the iguana king, he sent for\n his young son and said that such friendship was misplaced, for chameleons were\n low creatures, and that if the intimacy was persisted in, calamity would\n befall the whole of the tribe of iguanas. And he enjoined his son to have no\n more to do with the chameleon. But the son continued in his intimacy.”\n    -_Khuddaka Nikāya_, Jātaka 141 “Godha-jātaka”. ca. 4th cent. B.C.\n     trans. Robert Chalmers, 1895.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:jackal"
msgid "“Always ready to take advantage of every favourable opportunity, the Jackal is\n a sad parasite, and hangs on the skirts of the larger carnivora as they roam\n the country for prey, in the hope of securing some share of the creatures\n which they destroy or wound.”\n    -John George Wood, _The Illustrated Natural History: Mammalia_. 1865.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:jelly"
msgid "“Beware of the Blob!\n It creeps\n And leaps\n and glides and slides\n Across the floor\n Right through the door\n And all around the wall,\n A splotch, a blotch...”\n    -Burt Bacharach and Mack David, “Beware of the Blob”. 1958.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:killer klown"
msgid "“All the world loves a clown.”\n    -Cole Porter, “Be a Clown”. 1948.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:kobold"
msgid "“Kobolds are harmless.”\n    -Bearand the Bold, epitaph (Magic: the Gathering)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:komodo dragon"
msgid "“The three of us were sitting ashen faced as if we had just witnessed a foul\n and malignant murder. At least if we had been watching a murder the murderer\n wouldn't have been looking us impassively in the eye as he did it. Maybe it\n was the feeling of cold unflinching arrogance that so disturbed us. But\n whatever malign emotions we tried to pin on to the lizard, we knew that they\n weren't the lizard's emotions at all, only ours. The lizard was simply going\n about its lizardly business in a simple, straightforward lizardly way. It\n didn't know anything about the horror, the guilt, the shame, the ugliness that\n we, uniquely guilty and ashamed animals, were trying to foist on it. So we got\n it all straight back at us, as if reflected in the mirror of its single\n unwavering and disinterested eye.”\n    -Douglas Adams, “Last Chance to See”. 1990.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:kraken"
msgid "“... Kraken, also called the Crab-fish, which [according to the pilots of\n Norway] is not that huge, for heads and tails counted, he is no larger than\n our Öland is wide [i.e. less than 16 km] ... He stays at the sea floor,\n constantly surrounded by innumerable small fishes, who serve as his food and\n are fed by him in return: for his meal, if I remember correctly what E.\n Pontoppidan writes, lasts no longer than three months, and another three are\n then needed to digest it. His excrements nurture in the following an army of\n lesser fish, and for this reason, fishermen plumb after his resting place ...\n Gradually, Kraken ascends to the surface, and when he is at ten to twelve\n fathoms, the boats had better move out of his vicinity, as he will shortly\n thereafter burst up, like a floating island, spurting water from his dreadful\n nostrils and making ring waves around him, which can reach many miles. Could\n one doubt that this is the Leviathan of Job?”\n    -Jacob Wallenberg, “Min son på galejan”. 1781.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:large abomination"
msgid "<small abomination>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:lindwurm"
msgid "“Freilich verbürgt uns keine Silbe die Existenz von solcherlei Thieren, wenn\n wir uns den Drachen oder Lindwurm als ein Ungeheuer vorstellen, dessen langer\n Hals in einen Adler-, Löwen- oder Delphinenkopf endigt; das auf dem breiten\n Rücken Greifs- oder Nachisittige trägt; und am vielfach\n gerollten Schweif einen Stachel mit Widerhaken hat; Feuer speit; sich in\n Mädchen verliebt und diese entführt; bald diese bald jene Gestalt annimmt; auf\n sauer erworbenen Schatzen ruht — kurz, als ein Ungeheuer, das alle\n Eigenschaften besitzt, welche die Fabel ihm andichtet; dann wäre es Wahnsinn,\n an Drachen und Lindwürmer glauben zu wollen. Nehmen wir aber dafür bloß ein\n furchtbares Ungeheuer überhaupt, welches nun aus unserem Welttheile vertilgt\n ist, so hat der Glaube daran nichts Lächerliches.”\n    -Leopold Ziegelhauſer, _Schattenbilder der Vorzeit: Ein Kranz von\n     Geschichten, Sagen, Legenden, Märchen, Skizzen und Heldenmahlen, Aus allen\n     Gegenden Deutschlands und des österreichischen Kaiserstaates_. 1844.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:lost soul"
msgid "“She walks in the twilight, her steps make no sound,\n  Her feet leave no tracks on the dew-covered ground.\n Her hand gently beckons, she whispers your name—\n  But those who go with her are never the same.”\n    -Magic: the Gathering\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:manticore"
msgid "“Ctesias writeth, that in Aethiopia likewise there is a beast which he calleth\n Mantichora, having three rankes of teeth, which when they meet togither are\n let in one within another like the teeth of combes: with the face and eares of\n a man, with red eyes; of colour sanguine, bodied like a lyon, and having a\n taile armed with a sting like a scorpion: his voice resembleth the noise of a\n flute and trumpet sounded together: very swift he is, and mans flesh of all\n others hee most desireth.”\n    -Pliny the Elder, _Natural History_, Book 8, Chapter XXI\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:meliai"
msgid "“And Heaven came, bringing on night and longing for love, and he lay about Earth\n spreading himself full upon her. Then the son from his ambush stretched forth\n his left hand and in his right took the great long sickle with jagged teeth,\n and swiftly lopped off his own father's members and cast them away to fall\n behind him. And not vainly did they fall from his hand; for all the bloody\n drops that gushed forth Earth received, and as the seasons moved round she bare\n the strong Erinyes and the great Giants with gleaming armour, holding long\n spears in their hands, and the Nymphs whom they call Meliae all over the\n boundless earth.”\n    -Hesiod, _Theogony_, 182-187, trans. H.G. Evelyn-White. 1920\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:merfolk"
msgid "“Again at length my thought reviving came,\n When I no longer found my self the same;\n Then first this sea-green beard I felt to grow,\n And these large honours on my spreading brow;\n My long-descending locks the billows sweep,\n And my broad shoulders cleave the yielding deep;\n My fishy tail, my arms of azure hue,\n And ev'ry part divinely chang'd, I view.”\n    -Ovid, _Metamorphoses_, XIII, 546-7. 8 A.D.\n     trans. Garth, Dryden, et al.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:merfolk aquamancer"
msgid "“Lord, Lord! methought, what pain it was to drown!\n What dreadful noise of waters in mine ears!\n What ugly sights of death within mine eyes!\n Methought I saw a thousand fearful wrecks,\n Ten thousand men that fishes gnawed upon,\n Wedges of gold, great anchors, heaps of pearl,\n Inestimable stones, unvalued jewels,\n All scattered in the bottom of the sea:\n Some lay in dead men’s skulls; and in those holes\n Where eyes did once inhabit, there were crept,\n As ’t were in scorn of eyes, reflecting gems.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _King Richard III_, I, iv. 1597\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:merfolk avatar"
msgid "“Then up it raise the mermaiden,\n Wi the comb an glass in her hand:\n ‘Here's a health to you, my merrie young men,\n For you never will see dry land.’”\n    -“Sir Patrick Spens”, Scottish folk song, version 58J in Francis James\n     Child, _The English and Scottish Popular Ballads_. 1898.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:merfolk impaler"
msgid "“Damn ye! Let Neptune strike ye dead, Winslow! HAAARK! Hark Triton, hark!\n Bellow, bid our father the Sea King rise from the depths full foul in his fury!\n Black waves teeming with salt-foam to smother this young mouth with pungent\n slime, to choke ye, engorging your organs ‘til ye turn blue and bloated with\n bilge and brine and can scream no more -- only when he, crowned in cockle\n shells with slitherin’ tentacled tail and steaming beard take up his fell\n be-finnéd arm, his coral-tine trident screeches banshee-like in the tempest and\n plunges right through yer gullet, bursting ye -- a bulging bladder no more, but\n a blasted bloody film now -- a nothing for the harpies and the souls of dead\n sailors to peck and claw and feed upon, only to be lapped up and swallowed by\n the infinite waters of the dread emperor himself -- forgotten to any man, to\n any time, forgotten to any god or devil, forgotten even to the sea, for any\n stuff or part of Winslow, even any scantling of your soul is Winslow no more,\n but is now itself the sea!”\n    -Thomas Wake, _The Lighthouse_. 2019\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:merfolk javelineer"
msgid "<merfolk>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:merfolk siren"
msgid "“Row'd on, in reach of an erected voice,\n The Sirens soon took note, without our noise,\n Tuned those sweet accents that made charms so strong,\n And these learn'd numbers made the Sirens' song:\n ‘Come here, thou worthy of a world of praise,\n That dost so high the Grecian glory raise,\n Ulysses! stay thy ship, and that song hear\n That none past ever but it bent his ear,\n But left him ravish'd, and instructed more\n By us, than any ever heard before.\n For we know all things whatsoever were\n In wide Troy labour'd; whatsoever there\n The Grecians and the Trojans both sustain'd\n By those high issues that the Gods ordain'd.\n And whatsoever all the earth can show\n T' inform a knowledge of desert, we know.’”\n    -Homer, _The Odyssey_, XII, 268-82.\n     trans. George Chapman, 1857.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:molten gargoyle"
msgid "<war gargoyle>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:moth of wrath"
msgid "“When within sight of their foe Berserks wrought themselves into such a state\n of frenzy, that they bit their shields and rushed forward to the attack,\n throwing away their arms of defence, reckless of every danger, sometimes\n having nothing but a club, which carried with it death and destruction.”\n    -Paul Belloni Du Chaillu,_The Viking Age: the Early History, Manners, and\n     Customs of the Ancestors of the English Speaking Nations_. 1889.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:mummy"
msgid "“I see Egypt and the Egyptians — I see the pyramids and obelisks;\n I look on chisel'd histories, songs, philosophies, cut in slabs\n     of sand-stone, or on granite-blocks;\n I see at Memphis mummy-pits, containing mummies, embalm'd, swathed\n     in linen cloth, lying there many centuries;\n I look on the fall'n Theban, the large-ball'd eyes, the side-drooping\n     neck, the hands folded across the breast.”\n    -Walt Whitman, “Salut au Monde”\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:naga"
msgid "“Amongst the deities and Asuras and celestial Rishis, O amiable lady, the Nagas\n are endued with great energy. Possessed of great speed, they are endued again\n with excellent fragrance. They deserve to be worshipped. They are capable of\n granting boons. Indeed, we too deserve to be followed by others in our train.\n I tell thee, O lady, that we are incapable of being seen by human beings.”\n    -Mahābhārata, Santi Parva, Mokshadharma Parva, section CCCLX. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:naga mage"
msgid "<naga>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:nagaraja"
msgid "“Then there have also come\n nagas from Lake Nabhasa,\n Vesali, and Tacchaka.\n Kambalas, Assataras,\n Payagas, and their kin.\n And from the River Yamuna\n comes the prestigious naga, Dhatarattha.\n The great naga Eravanna:\n He, too, has come\n to the forest meeting.”\n    -Mahasamaya Sutta, _Dīgha Nikāya_, 20. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Thanissaro Bhikkhu\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:naga warrior"
msgid "<naga>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:nameless horror"
msgid "“Then the sallow oval between Ged's arms grew bright. It widened and spread, a\n rent in the darkness of the earth and night, a ripping open of the fabric of\n the world. Through it blazed a terrible brightness. And through that bright\n misshapen breach clambered something like a clot of black shadow, quick and\n hideous, and it leaped straight out at Ged's face.”\n    -Ursula K. Le Guin, _A Wizard Of Earthsea_. 1968.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:necromancer"
msgid "“Even now the intensity of his dread power can be felt\n Weakening the body and saddening the heart\n Ultimately they will become empty, mindless spectres\n Stripped of will and soul\n Only their thirst for freedom\n Gives them hunger for vengeance...”\n    -Rush, “The Necromancer”. 1975.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:nekomata"
msgid "“Humans, eh? Think they’re lords of creation. Not like us cats. We _know_ we\n are. Ever see a cat feed a human? Case proven.”\n    -Terry Pratchett, “The Amazing Maurice and His Educated Rodents”. 2001.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:neqoxec"
msgid "“If thus mutation is influenced by natural selection, it implies, that any\n particular mutation must advance in a direction advantageous for the\n respective species, and, indeed, many examples of mutation known among fossil\n animals are apparently due to the advantage produced by the change. I must add\n here, however that probably not all mutations (in a palaeontological meaning)\n are due to natural selection, but that many do not imply an actual\n improvement.”\n    -_Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society_, Volume XXV, no. 150.\n     1896.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:occultist"
msgid "“Each family or tribe has a wizard or conjuring doctor, whose office we could\n never clearly ascertain.”\n    -Charles Darwin, _The Voyage of the Beagle_, ch. X. 1839.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:octopode"
msgid "“In their brief time together Slothrop forms the impression that this octopus\n is not in good mental health, though where's his basis for comparing? But\n there is a mad exhuberance, as with inanimate objects which fall off of tables\n when we are sensitive to noise and our own clumsiness and don't want them to\n fall, a sort of wham! ha-ha you hear that? here it is again, WHAM! in the\n cephalopod's every movement, which Slothrop is glad to get away from as he\n finally scales the crab like a discus, with all his strength, out to sea, and\n the octopus, with an eager splash and gurgle, strikes out in pursuit, and is\n presently gone.”\n    -Thomas Pynchon, _Gravity's Rainbow_. 1973.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ogre"
msgid "“The little princess, asleep in her cradle, floated on the water, and at last\n she was cast up on the shore of a beautiful country, where, however, very few\n people dwelt since the ogre Ravagio and his wife Tourmentine had gone to live\n there-for they ate up everybody. Ogres are terrible people. When once they\n have tasted raw human flesh they will hardly eat anything else, and\n Tourmentine always knew how to make some body come their way, for she was half\n a fairy.”\n    -Marie-Catherine Le Jumel de Barneville, Baronne d'Aulnoy, “'Orangier et\nl'Abeille”. 1697.\n\n“NO. Layers. Onions have layers. Ogres have layers. Onions have layers. You get\n it? We both have layers.”\n    -Shrek. 2001.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ogre mage"
msgid "<ogre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:oklob plant"
msgid "“Carbonic acid is one of the three materials which together form the starting\n point of vegetable growth; the others being water and nitric acid. This acid is\n formed of carbon and oxygen in the proportion of one part of the former to\n two of the latter chemically combined. It is a colorless gas, having an acid\n taste and smell; is soluble in water; weighs one-half more than air and can be\n poured from one vessel to another, as a liquid may be; 100 parts of water\n dissolve 106 parts of this gas, and it is from this source that the roots of\n plants derive the needed supplies of it.”\n    -Henry Stuart, _The Culture of Farm Crops: A Manual of the Science of\n    Agriculture, and a Hand-book of Practice for American Farmers_, ch X. 1887.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:oklob sapling"
msgid "<oklob plant>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:oni incarcerator"
msgid "“The popularity and longevity of the oni myth is no doubt partially based on\n the beings’ conventional demonic accoutrements, which have remained\n relatively constant through the ages: they are dreadful super-natural\n beings emerging from the abyss of Buddhist hell to terrify wicked mortals;\n their grotesque and savage demeanor and form instill instant fear; and the\n oni’s omnipresence in the socio-historical and cultural archive of Japan is\n directly attributable to the moral, social, and religious edification that\n stories about oni engender.”\n    -Noriko T. Reider, _Japanese Demon Lore: Oni from Ancient Times to the\n     Present_, ch. 1, 2010.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ophan"
msgid "“As I looked at the living creatures, I saw a wheel on the ground beside\n each creature with its four faces. This was the appearance and structure\n of the wheels: They sparkled like chrysolite, and all four looked alike.\n Each appeared to be made like a wheel intersecting a wheel. As they\n moved, they would go in any one of the four directions the creatures faced;\n the wheels did not turn about as the creatures went. Their rims were\n high and awesome, and all four rims were full of eyes all around.”\n    -Ezekiel 1:15-18 (New International Version)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:orange demon"
msgid "“In Sparkill buried lies that man of mark\n Who brought the Obelisk to Central Park,\n Redoubtable Commander H.H. Gorringe,\n Whose name supplies the long-sought rhyme for ‘orange.’”\n    -Arthur Guiterman, “Local Note”. 1934.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:orb guardian"
msgid "“X gon' give it to ya (what)\n Fuck waitin' for you to get it on your own, X gon' deliver to ya\n Knock knock, open up the door, it's real\n With the non-stop pop-pop from stainless steel\n [...]\n First we gonna rock, then we gonna roll\n Then we let it pop, go, let it go\n X gon' give it to ya, he gon' give it to ya\n X gon' give it to ya, he gon' give it to ya\n    -DMX, “X Gon' Give It To Ya”. 2002\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:orb of fire"
msgid "“There 's not the smallest orb which thou behold'st\n But in his motion like an angel sings,\n Still quiring to the young-eyed cherubins.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Merchant of Venice_, V, i. 1597.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:pandemonium lord"
msgid "“Mean while the winged heralds by command\n Of Sovran power, with awful Ceremony\n And Trumpets sound throughout the Host proclaim\n A solemn Councel forthwith to be held\n At Pandaemonium, the high Capital\n Of Satan and his Peers...”\n    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book I, 1667.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:phantom"
msgid "“Who wondrous things concerning our welfare, And straunge phantomes doth lett\n us ofte foresee.”\n    -Spenser, _The Faerie Queene_ II. xii. 47\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:pillar of salt"
msgid "“Then the LORD rained upon Sodom and upon Gomorrah brimstone and fire from the\n LORD out of heaven; And he overthrew those cities, and all the plain, and all\n the inhabitants of the cities, and that which grew upon the ground. But\n [Lot's] wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt.”\n    -KJV Bible, Genesis 19:24-26.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:player ghost"
msgid "“Know thyself.”\n    -Inscription in the pronaos of the Temple of Apollo at Delphi\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:polar bear"
msgid "“The Polar Bear is an animal of tremendous strength and fierceness. Barentz, in\n his voyage in search of a north-east passage to China, had proofs of the\n ferocity of these animals, in the island of Nova Zembla, where they attacked\n his seamen, seizing them in their mouths; carrying them off with the utmost\n ease, and devouring them in the sight of their comrades. It is said that they\n will attack and attempt to board armed vessels, at a great distance from\n shore, and have sometimes been with much difficulty repelled.”\n    -George Shaw, _General Zoology, or, Systematic Natural History_, vol. I,\n     p. 2. 1800.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:profane servitor"
msgid "“A spell was cast and the sky turned red\n The angel's heart froze to ice\n In the gloomy sky black clouds were gathering\n The silence was broken by cries\n A spell was cast and the sky turned red\n The angel's heart froze to ice\n In the gloomy sky — The silence where dead angels lie\n\n Touch the snow... Caress the lifeless sculptures\n Die!!!”\n    -Dissection, “Where Dead Angels Lie”. 1996.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:program bug"
msgid "“If builders built buildings the way programmers wrote programs, then the first\n woodpecker that came along would destroy civilization.”\n    -Gerald Weinberg, Weinberg's Second Law\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:protean progenitor"
msgid "“I call upon Proteus,\n key-holding master of the sea,\n first-born, who showed\n the beginnings of all nature,\n changing matter\n into a great variety of forms.\n Honored by all, he is wise,\n and he knows what is now,\n what was before,\n and what will be in the future.\n He has all at his disposal,\n transformed far beyond\n all other immortals\n who dwell on snowy Olympos\n and fly through the air\n and over land and sea,\n for Physis was the first to place\n everything in Proteus.\n Father, attended by holy providence,\n visit the mystic initiates,\n and bring a good end to a life\n of industry and prosperity.”\n    -_The Orphic Hymns_, XXV.\n     trans. Apostolos N. Athanassakis and Benjamin M. Wolkow, 2013.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:rakshasa"
msgid "“Vaisampayana said, 'Not far from the place where the Pandavas were asleep, a\n Rakshasa by name Hidimva dwelt on the Sala tree. Possessed of great energy and\n prowess, he was a cruel cannibal of visage that was grim in consequence of his\n sharp and long teeth. He was now hungry and longing for human flesh. Of long\n shanks and a large belly, his locks and beard were both red in hue. His\n shoulders were broad like the neck of a tree; his ears were like unto arrows,\n and his features were frightful.”\n    -_Mahābhārata_, Adi Parva, Hidimva-vadha Parva, section CLIV. ca. 500 B.C.\n     trans. Kisari Mohan Ganguli, 1883.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:reaper"
msgid "“All our times have come.\n Here but now they're gone.\n Seasons don't fear the reaper,\n Nor do the wind, the sun or the rain.\n We can be like they are.\n Come on baby, don't fear the reaper.”\n\n    -Buck Dharma, Blue Öyster Cult, “(Don't Fear) The Reaper”. 1976.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ribbon worm"
msgid "“While the angels, all pallid and wan,\n   Uprising, unveiling, affirm\n That the play is the tragedy, ‘Man’,\n   And its hero, the Conqueror Worm.”\n    -Edgar Allan Poe\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:royal mummy"
msgid "<mummy>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:salamander"
msgid "“As for example: the Salamander made in fashion of a Lizard, marked with spots\n like to stars, never comes abroad and sheweth it selfe but in great showers;\n for in faire weather he is not seene. He is of so cold a complexion, that if\n hee do but touch the fire, hee wil quench it as presently, as if yce were put\n into it. The Salamander casteth up at the mouth a certaine venomous matter\n like unto milke, let it but once touch any bare part of a man or womans bodie,\n all the haire will fall off: and the part so touched will change the colour of\n the skin to the white morphew.”\n    -Gaius Plinius Secundus, _Naturalis Historia_, Book X, ch. LXVII. 79 A.D.\n     trans. Philemon Holland, 1601.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scorpion"
msgid "“Those poisonous fields, with rank luxuriance crown'd,\n Where the dark scorpion gathers death around”\n    -Oliver Goldsmith, “The Deserted Village”, l. 351-2. 1770.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:seraph"
msgid "“In the year that king Uzziah died I saw also the LORD sitting upon a throne,\n high and lifted up, and his train filled the temple.\n\n Above it stood the seraphims: each one had six wings; with twain he covered\n his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly.\n\n And one cried unto another, and said, Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts:\n the whole earth is full of his glory.”\n    -KJV Bible, Isaiah 6:1-3.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shadowghast"
msgid "“Between the idea\n And the reality\n Between the motion\n And the act\n Falls the Shadow\n     For thine is the Kingdom\n\n Between the conception\n And the creation\n Between the emotion\n And the response\n Falls the Shadow\n     Life is very long”\n    -T.S. Eliot, “The Hollow Men”. 1925.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shadow demon"
msgid "“As we grow old, we become aware that death is drawing near; his shadow falls\n across our path...”\n    -Stefan Zweig, _Twenty-Four Hours in the Life of a Woman_. 1927.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shadow dragon"
msgid "“Crux sacra sit mihi lux / Non draco sit mihi dux\n Vade retro satana / Numquam suade mihi vana\n Sunt mala quae libas / Ipse venena bibas\n\n [Let the Holy Cross be my light / Let not the dragon be my guide\n  Step back Satan / Never tempt me with vain things\n  What you offer me is evil / You drink the poison yourself.]”\n    -Full text of initials on the Saint Benedict Medal, worn to ward off evil\n     spirits. Inscription from 11-15th century, formally approved by Pope\n     Benedict XIV in 18th century.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shadow wraith"
msgid "“Yesterday, upon the stair,\n I met a man who wasn't there\n He wasn't there again today\n I wish, I wish he'd go away...”\n    -Hughes Mearns ,_Antigonish_, 1-4. 1899.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shambling mangrove"
msgid "“There is unrest in the forest\n There is trouble with the trees\n For the maples want more sunlight\n And the oaks ignore their pleas”\n    -Rush, \"The Trees\". 1978.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shapeshifter"
msgid "“And then th' old Sea-God crept\n From forth the deeps, and found his fat calves there,\n Survey'd, and number'd, and came never near\n The craft we used, but told us five for calves.\n His temples then dis-eased with sleep he salves;\n And in rush'd we, with an abhorred cry,\n Cast all our hands about him manfully;\n And then th' old Forger all his forms began:\n First was a lion with a mighty mane,\n Then next a dragon, a pied panther then,\n A vast boar next, and suddenly did strain\n All into water. Last he was a tree,\n Curl'd all at top, and shot up to the sky.”\n    -Homer, _The Odyssey_, IV, 602-14.\n     trans. George Chapman, 1857.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:silent spectre"
msgid "“There is a silence where hath been no sound,\n There is a silence where no sound may be,—\n In the cold grave, under the deep, deep sea,\n Or in the wide desert where no life is found.”\n    -Thomas Hood, “Silence”. Early 19th cent.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:simulacrum"
msgid "“The simulacrum now hides, not the truth, but the fact that there is none, that\n is to say, the continuation of Nothingness.”\n    -Jean Baudrillard, “Radical Thought”. 1994.\n     trans. François Debrix, 1995.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:sixfirhy"
msgid "“I saw a mouth jeering. A smile of melted red iron ran over it. Its laugh was\n full of nails rattling. It was a child's dream of a mouth.\n A fist hit the mouth: knuckles of gun-metal driven by an electric wrist and\n shoulder. It was a child's dream of an arm.\n The fist hit the mouth over and over, again and again. The mouth bled melted\n iron, and laughed its laughter of nails rattling.\n And I saw the more the fist pounded the more the mouth laughed. The fist is\n pounding and pounding, and the mouth answering.”\n    -Carl Sandburg, “Gargoyle”, _Cornhusker_. 1918.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:skeletal warrior"
msgid "“Speak! speak! thou fearful guest!\n Who, with thy hollow breast\n Still in rude armor drest,\n Comest to daunt me!\n Wrapt not in Eastern balms,\n But with thy fleshless palms\n Stretched, as if asking alms,\n Why dost thou haunt me?”\n    -Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, “The Skeleton in Armor”. 1841.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:skeleton"
msgid "“God save us from the skeleton\n Who sitteth at the feast!”\n    -James Jeffrey Roche, _The Skeleton at the Feast_. 1890.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:sky beast"
msgid "“Her own mother lived the latter years of her life in the horrible suspicion\n that electricity was dripping invisibly all over the house.”\n    -James Thurber, _My Life and Hard Times_. 1934.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:slime creature"
msgid "“The very deep did rot: O Christ!\n That ever this should be!\n Yea, slimy things did crawl with legs\n Upon the slimy sea.”\n    -Samuel Tayor Coleridge, “The Rime of the Ancient Mariner”. 1798.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:small abomination"
msgid "“No — it wasn't that way at all. It was everywhere — a gelatin — a slime yet it\n had shapes, a thousand shapes of horror beyond all memory. There were eyes —\n and a blemish. It was the pit — the maelstrom — the ultimate abomination.\n Carter, it was the unnamable!”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft, _The Unnamable_. 1925.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:smoke demon"
msgid "“Why does the thin grey strand\n Floating up from the forgotten\n Cigarette between my fingers,\n Why does it trouble me?”\n    -D.H. Lawrence, “Sorrow”. 1916.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:snapping turtle"
msgid "“After a while they came to a village. ”Now then,“ said Snapping Turtle, ”in\n the morning at daylight, my friends, we will make on attack. I myself will\n first go to the place,“ the leader of the war party said to them.\n\n ”Good,“ said the other little one, ”thou art the one who sees to it what we\n shall do,“ they said to that Snapping Turtle. ”Now then,“ said Snapping\n Turtle, ”verily I am now going to tell you what I shall do.“ Thus he spoke.\n ”Now is the time I shall begin to walk toward this village. Verily at the time\n I shall kill the daughter of the chief will be when the light of day is\n breaking, and at the same instant the sky will glow with red in the direction\n whence the morrow comes. ‘Ho, there, our comrade has killed her!’ will thus be\n the thought in your hearts. Then is the time when you want to make a great\n noise, when you shall whoop all keep it up. Now is the time that you go to\n attack this village.“ Thus he spoke to those his young men.\n\n ”All right!“ said the other little fellows.”\n    -“When Snapping Turtle went to War”, _Publications of the American\n     Ethnological Society, Volume IX: Kickapoo Tales_. 1915.\n     trans. Truman Michelson\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:soul eater"
msgid "“Negation is the mind's first freedom.”\n    -E.M. Cioran, _The Temptation to Exist_. 1956.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:spatial vortex"
msgid "“It was just a colour out of space—a frightful messenger from unformed realms\n of infinity beyond all Nature as we know it; from realms whose mere existence\n stuns the brain and numbs us with the black extra-cosmic gulfs it throws open\n before our frenzied eyes.”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft, “The Colour out of Space”. 1927.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:spatial maelstrom"
msgid "“In any composition, the solid narrates the anomalies generated by the void, or\n the infection of the void through the solid (where the void comes to the solid,\n it works as a convoluting plague, a coiling swirling epidemic rather than a\n nullifying process or a solid-annihilating agent); in a composition there is no\n pure solid but a defiled one, a diseased and deflowered solid.\n [...]\n The only way that the solid can initialize its architectonic and compositional\n activities (processes for survival, development, etc.) is by letting the void\n in. The dynamic traits of solid can only be actuated when solid is eaten,\n convoluted and messed up by the void. There is no other option for solid.”\n    -Reza Negarestani, _Cyclonopedia: Complicity with Anonymous Materials_. 2008\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:spectral thing"
msgid "“On the hungry craving wind\n My Spectre follows thee behind.”\n    -William Blake, “Broken Love”. ca. 1800.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:spellspark servitor"
msgid "“When you think\n Your toys have gone berserk\n It's an illusion\n You cannot shirk”\n    -Siouxsie and the Banshees, “Spellbound”\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:sphinx marauder"
msgid "“Oedipus speaks: 'Far from me is the crime and shame of cowardice, and my\n valour knows not dastard fears[...] The Sphinx, weaving her words in darkling\n measures, I fled not; I faced the bloody jaws of the fell prophetess and the\n ground white with scattered bones. And when from a lofty cliff, already\n hovering over her prey, she prepared her pinions and, lashing her tail like\n a savage lion, stirred up her threatening wrath, I asked her riddle.\n Thereupon came a sound of dread; her jaws crashed, and her talons, brooking\n no delay, eager for my vitals, tore at the rocks. The lot's intricate,\n guile-entangled words, the grim riddle of the winged beast, I solved.'”\n    -Seneca, _Oedipus_, 1st cent. A.D.\n     trans. Frank Justus Miller, 1917.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:star jelly"
msgid "\"My colleagues may have dismissed the sightings as blink frog egg casings, but\ntoday I shall prove that these creatures came from beyond the firmament. Let's\nbegin the dissection.\"\n        -Ardun Idrick, cryptozoologist, last words\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:starcursed mass"
msgid "“Did you see that star go out?\n I seen it burn.\n\n That little star went out,\n your little eyes went out.\n\n Our burnt little dreams are hid\n up where the stars get lit.”\n    -Thee Silver Mt. Zion, “I Built Myself a Metal Bird”. 2010.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:stone giant"
msgid "“I really believe what you say, answered the knight; for, I have been engaged\n with the giant, in the most obstinate and outrageous combat that I believe I\n shall ever fight in all the days of my life: with one backstroke, slam went\n his head to the ground; and discharged such a quantity of blood, that it ran\n like rills of water, along the field.”\n    -Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra, _The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La\n     Mancha_, IV, 10. 1605.\n     trans. Carlos Fuentes, 1997.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:sun demon"
msgid "“Behold him setting in his western skies,\n The shadows lengthening as the vapours rise.”\n    -John Dryden, “Absalom and Achitophel”, I, 268. 1681.\n\n“The sky is sad and beautiful, like a vast altar.\n The sun has drowned in its congealing blood.”\n    -Charles Baudelaire, _Flowers of Evil_, 43: Evening Harmony. 1857.\n     trans. Ruth White, 1969.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:swamp drake"
msgid "“Sweet is the swamp with its secrets,\n Until we meet a snake;\n 'T is then we sigh for houses,\n And our departure take\n\n At that enthralling gallop\n That only childhood knows.\n A snake is summer's treason,\n And guile is where it goes.”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “A Snake”. ca. 1865.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:swamp worm"
msgid "“The fool, as I think, at the chasm's brink,\n Prone by the swamp or the marsh's side,\n Did, even as I, in the end rejoice,\n Since the voice of death must be His true voice.”\n    -Arthur Edward Waite, “At The End of Things”. 1906.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:tengu"
msgid "“A Bird came down the Walk —\n He did not know I saw —\n He bit an Angleworm in halves\n And ate the fellow, raw”\n    -Emily Dickinson, “A Bird came down the Walk”. ca. 1865.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:tengu warrior"
msgid "“I can puff up my feathers, look real mean\n Be the old man that this here's scene\n I'm a struttin' preenin' bantam rooster looking for a fight.”\n    -Andrew Bird's Bowl of Fire, “Cock o' the Walk”. 1998.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:tentacled monstrosity"
msgid "“Oozing and surging up out of that yawning trap-door in the Cyclopean crypt I\n had glimpsed such an unbelievable behemothic monstrosity that I could not\n doubt the power of its original to kill with its mere sight. Even now I cannot\n begin to suggest it with any words at my command. I might call it gigantic —\n tentacled — proboscidian — octopus-eyed — semi-amorphous — plastic — partly\n squamous and partly rugose — ugh!”\n    -H.P. Lovecraft and Hazel Heald, “Out of the Aeons”, _Weird Tales_, 25, No.\n     4, pp. 478-96. April 1935.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:titan"
msgid "“And on the other part the Titans eagerly strengthened their ranks, and both\n sides at one time showed the work of their hands and their might. The\n boundless sea rang terribly around, and the earth crashed loudly: wide Heaven\n was shaken and groaned, and high Olympus reeled from its foundation under the\n charge of the undying gods, and a heavy quaking reached dim Tartarus and the\n deep sound of their feet in the fearful onset and of their hard missiles. So,\n then, they launched their grievous shafts upon one another, and the cry of\n both armies as they shouted reached to starry heaven; and they met together\n with a great battle-cry.”\n    -Hesiod, _Theogony_, 8th cent. B.C.\n     trans. H.G. Evelyn-White, 1914.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:toadstool"
msgid "“But the Seneschal gathered the toadstool fly-bane.”\n    -Adam Mickiewicz, _Pan Tadeusz_, III. 1834.\n     trans. G.R. Noyes, 1917.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:toenail golem"
msgid "“Gentle socks pamper them by day, and shoes cobbled of leather fortify them,\n but my toes hardly notice. All they're interested in is turning out\n toenails—semitransparent, flexible sheets of a hornlike material, as defence\n against—whom?”\n    -Jorge Luis Borges, “Toenails”. 1960.\n    trans. Andrew Hurley, 1998.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:tormentor"
msgid "“Thou art to me a delicious torment.”\n    -Ralph Waldo Emerson, _Essays: First Series_, Essay VI: Friendship. 1841.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:torpor snail"
msgid "“Snail, snail, slug-slow,\n To me thy four horns show;\n If thou dost not show me thy four,\n I will throw thee out of the door,\n For the crow in the gutter,\n To eat for bread and butter.”\n    -Silesian rhyme. Quoted by S.W. Singer in _Notes and Queries_, no. 69.\n     1851.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:training dummy"
msgid "“Things are only mannequins and even the great world-historical events are only\n costumes beneath which they exchange glances with nothingness, with the base\n and the banal.”\n    -Walter Benjamin, _Protocols to the Experiments on Hashish, Opium and\n     Mescaline 1927-1934_, “Protocol II: Highlights of the Second Hashish\n     Impression”. 15 January 1928.\n     trans. Scott J. Thompson, 1997.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:two-headed ogre"
msgid "<ogre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:tyrant leech"
msgid " Scully: “Where the hell did it come from?”\n Mulder: “I don’t know, but it looks like I’m going to have to tell Skinner\n          that his suspect is a giant bloodsucking worm after all.”\n    -_The X-Files_, “The Host”. 1994\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ufetubus"
msgid "“We were permitted to shriek in the tongue of dwarfs and demons”\n    -Czesław Miłosz, “A Task”.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ugly thing"
msgid "“Beauty is no quality in things themselves: It exists merely in the mind which\n contemplates them; and each mind perceives a different beauty. One person may\n even perceive deformity, where another is sensible of beauty; and every\n individual ought to acquiesce in his own sentiment, without pretending to\n regulate those of others.”\n    -David Hume\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:unseen horror"
msgid "“The awful shadow of some unseen Power\n Floats, tho’ unseen, amongst us.”\n    -Percy Bysshe Shelley, “Hymn to Intellectual Beauty”, I, 1-2. 1816.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ushabti"
msgid "“Spell for causing a shabti to do work for a man in the realm of the dead:\n O shabti, allotted to me, if I be summoned or if I be detailed to do any work\n which has to be done in the realm of the dead, if indeed any obstacles are\n implanted for you therewith as a man at his duties, you shall detail yourself\n for me on every occasion of making arable the fields, of flooding the banks or\n of conveying sand from east to west; ‘Here I am’, you shall say.\n\n    -Spell 6, _Book of the Dead_, from _Ancient Egyptian Book of the Dead:\n     Journey through the afterlife_, edited by John H. Taylor. 2010\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:vampire"
msgid " • Scuze me. My friend Burt says you're a vampire hunter. 'zat so?\n — Uh yes. Yes it is.\n • Well you n'ain't gonna find one round here, mate. They ain't no vampires.\n — You don't believe in vampires?\n • 'course not!\n • But I do believe in con artists. And charletans who like to stir up trouble!\n • Dead people who get up at night and suck blood. How stupid do you think we\n   are?\n • My place is haunted with 16 ghosts and they all say there n'ain't no\n   vampires!\n    -Rich Morris, Yet Another Fantasy Gamer Comic\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:vampire bat"
msgid "“For something is amiss and out of place\n When mice with wings can wear a human face.”\n    -Theodore Roethke, “The Bat”. 1938.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:very ugly thing"
msgid "“I'm ugly, I'm ugly as sin,\n But beautiful's out, ugly's in.\n If you're ugly like me, you're in good company.\n There are millions of us who are ugly.”\n    -_The Muppet Show_, “Ugly Song”. 1977\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:vine stalker"
msgid "“The monstrous plant bud . . . had grown again with preternatural rapidity,\n from Falmer's head. A loathsome pale-green stem was mounting thickly, and had\n started to branch like antlers after attaining a height of six or seven\n inches.\n\n More dreadful than this, if possible, similar growths had issued from the\n eyes; and their stems, climbing vertically across the forehead, had entirely\n displaced the eyeballs.”\n    -Clark Ashton Smith, “The Seed from the Sepulcher”. 1933.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:wandering mushroom"
msgid "“Telimena, wearied with the prolonged wrangling, wanted to go out into the\n fresh air, but sought a partner. She took a little basket from the peg.\n “Gentlemen, I see that you wish to remain within doors,” she said, wrapping\n around her head a red cashmere shawl, “but I am going for mushrooms: follow me\n who will!” Under one arm she took the little daughter of the Chamberlain, with\n the other she raised her skirt up to her ankles. Thaddeus silently hastened\n after her—to seek mushrooms!”\n    -Adam Mickiewicz, _Pan Tadeusz_, II. 1834.\n     trans. G.R. Noyes, 1917.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:war gargoyle"
msgid "“Their innumerable sculptures of demons and dragons assumed a lugubrious\n aspect. The restless light of the flame made them move to the eye. There were\n griffins which had the air of laughing, gargoyles which one fancied one heard\n yelping, salamanders which puffed at the fire, tarasques which sneezed in the\n smoke. And among the monsters thus roused from their sleep of stone by this\n flame, by this noise, there was one who walked about, and who was seen, from\n time to time, to pass across the glowing face of the pile, like a bat in front\n of a candle.”\n    -Victor Hugo, _The Hunchback of Notre-Dame_, 10, ch. IV. 1831.\n     trans. Isabel F. Hapgood\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:weeping skull"
msgid "“Alas, poor Yorick! I knew him, Horatio, a fellow of infinite jest, of most\n excellent fancy. He hath bore me on his back a thousand times, and now how\n abhorr'd in my imagination it is! My gorge rises at it.”\n    -William Shakespeare, _The Tragedy of Hamlet, Prince of Denmark_, V, 1. 1603\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:wolf"
msgid "“Teacher: How does a dog smell?\n Student: I don't know.\n Teacher: Correct!”\n    -Traditional Spanish joke.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:water nymph"
msgid "“Oh, but you can't expect to wield supreme executive power just because some\n watery tart threw a sword at you.”\n    -Monty Python and the Holy Grail, 1975.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:wight"
msgid "“Unhappie wight, borne to desastrous end,\n That doth his life in so long tendance spend!”\n    -Edmund Spenser, “Mother Hubberds Tale”, _Complaints_. 1591.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:worm"
msgid "<ribbon worm>\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:wraith"
msgid "“God, though this life is but a wraith,\n Although we know not what we use,\n Although we grope with little faith,\n Give me the heart to fight—and lose.”\n    -Louis Untermeyer, “Prayer”. 1919.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:wretched star"
msgid "“Stare into the sun\n Wherever you may roam\n Feel it calling you home\n Despite the mounting danger\n Stare into the sun\n Let its fire fill you\n Whatever doesn’t kill you\n Only makes you stranger”\n    -The Sunbeams, “The Tug”. 2020\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:wyrmhole"
msgid "“My complete answer to the late 19th century question 'what is electrodynamics\n trying to tell us' would simply be this:\n\n Fields in empty space have physical reality; the medium that supports them does\n not.\n\n Having thus removed the mystery from electrodynamics, let me immediately do the\n same for quantum mechanics:\n\n Correlations have physical reality; that which they correlate does not.”\n    -N. David Mermin, _What is quantum mechanics trying to tell us?_, 1998.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ynoxinul"
msgid "“He fixed his eyes upon the door, which, slowly opening, disclosed a stranger\n of majestic form, but scowling features, who demanded sternly, why he was\n summoned? ‘I did not summon you,’ said the trembling student. ‘You did!’ said\n the stranger, advancing, angrily; ‘and the demons are not to be invoked in\n vain.’ The student could make no reply; and the demon, enraged that one of the\n uninitiated should have summoned him out of mere presumption, seized him by\n the throat and strangled him.\n\n When Agrippa returned, a few days afterwards, he found his house beset with\n devils. Some of them were sitting on the chimneypots, kicking up their legs in\n the air; while others were playing at leapfrog, on the very edge of the\n parapet. His study was so filled with them that he found it difficult to make\n his way to his desk.”\n    -Charles Mackay, _Memoirs of Extraordinary Popular Delusions_, Vol. III,\n     Part I. 1841.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/quotes.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:zombie"
msgid "“It seemed that while the zombie came from the grave, it was neither a ghost,\n nor yet a person who had been raised like Lazarus from the dead. The zombie,\n they say, is a soulless human corpse, still dead, but taken from the grave and\n endowed by sorcery with a mechanical semblance of life—it is a dead body which\n is made to walk and act and move as if it were alive. People who have the\n power to do this go to a fresh grave, dig up the body before it has had time\n to rot, galvanize it into movement, and then make of it a servant or slave,\n occasionally for the commission of some crime, more often simply as a drudge\n around the habitation or the farm, setting it dull heavy tasks, and beating it\n like a dumb beast if it slackens.”\n    -William Seabrook, _The Magic Island_. 1929.\n\n“They’re not afraid! No matter what we do, no matter how many we kill, they will\n never, ever be afraid!”\n    -Max Brooks, _World War Z: An Oral History of the Zombie War_. 2006.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:tough skin mutation"
msgid "Your epidermis has hardened, providing a small amount of protection. Each level\nof this mutation provides +1 AC.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:shaggy fur mutation"
msgid "You are covered in fur, providing a small amount of protection. Each level of\nthis mutation provides +1 AC. The third level of this mutation also provides\nresistance to cold.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:repulsion field mutation"
msgid "You are surrounded by a repulsion field, providing a small amount of evasion.\nEach level of this mutation provides more EV. The third level of this mutation\nalso repels missiles, greatly increasing your EV against such attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:icy blue scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in blue scales, providing a small amount of protection. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides more AC. The third level of this mutation also\nprovides resistance to cold.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:molten scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in molten scales, providing a small amount of protection. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides more AC. The third level of this mutation also\nprovides resistance to fire.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:slimy green scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in green scales, providing a small amount of protection. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides more AC. The third level of this mutation also\nprovides resistance to poison.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:yellow scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in yellow scales, providing a small amount of protection. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides more AC. The third level of this mutation also\nprovides resistance to acid and corrosion.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:thin metallic scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in metallic scales, providing a small amount of protection.\nEach level of this mutation provides more AC. The third level of this mutation\nalso provides resistance to electricity.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:rugged brown scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in brown scales, providing a small amount of protection. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides +1 AC and also increases your health slightly.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:sharp scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in sharp scales, providing a small amount of protection. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides +1 AC and Slay +1, improving your accuracy and\ndamage with melee and ranged attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:iridescent scales mutation"
msgid "You are covered in iridescent scales, providing a small amount of protection.\nEach level of this mutation provides +2 AC.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:large bone plates mutation"
msgid "You are covered in bone plates, providing a small amount of shielding. Each\nlevel of this mutation provides more SH.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:thin skeletal structure mutation"
msgid "Your bones are light and porous. Each level of this mutation provides +2\nDexterity and Stealth+.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:strong mutation"
msgid "Your muscles have grown unnaturally strong. Each level of this mutation provides\n+4 Strength, but the extra muscles slow your movements and thoughts, giving -1 to\nboth your Intelligence and Dexterity for each level.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:clever mutation"
msgid "Your mind has become unnaturally acute. Each level of this mutation provides +4\nIntelligence, but all the blood flowing to your head weakens your constitution,\ngiving -1 to both your Strength and Dexterity for each level.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:agile mutation"
msgid "Your reflexes have become unnaturally fast. Each level of this mutation\nprovides +4 Dexterity, but the rest of your body can't keep up with your speed,\ngiving -1 to both your Strength and Intelligence for each level.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:weak mutation"
msgid "Your muscles have grown unnaturally weak. Each level of this mutation provides\n-3 Strength.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:dopey mutation"
msgid "Your mind has become unnaturally dopey. Each level of this mutation provides -3\nIntelligence.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:clumsy mutation"
msgid "Your reflexes have become unnaturally clumsy. Each level of this mutation\nprovides -3 Dexterity.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:high mp mutation"
msgid "You have an increased reservoir of magic. Each level of this mutation provides\n+10% MP.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:low mp mutation"
msgid "You have a low magical capacity. Each level of this mutation provides -10% MP.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:efficient magic mutation"
msgid "You have both an innate understanding and a greater control over the exact flow\nof your magic, letting you cast spells with greater efficiency. Each level of\nthis mutation reduces the cost of your spells by 1 MP, to a minimum of 1 MP.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:ephemeral shield mutation"
msgid "Whenever you use spells or divine abilities with any costs, a shield forms from\nthe excess loose magical or spiritual energy to guard you. The magical energy\nfrom spells only sustains the shield for but a moment, while the spiritual\nenergy from using divine abilities lasts a brief bit longer.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:camouflage mutation"
msgid "Your skin changes colour to match your surroundings. Each level of this\nmutation provides Stealth+.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:passive freeze mutation"
msgid "Foes who strike you in melee will be frozen, taking damage. This effect is\nguaranteed to trigger if your foe does any damage.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:time-warped blood mutation"
msgid "Your blood has desynchronised itself from the flow of time. Whenever you fall\nbelow 50% of your maximum HP, the spilling of your blood surges strangely\nthrough your surroundings, hasting two of your strongest allies in sight for\neach level of this mutation. After doing so, your blood will be unable to\nfurther dilate time until your HP has fully recovered.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:efficient metabolism mutation"
msgid "Your altered digestive system extracts additional value out of select potion\ntypes, doubling the duration of all status effects gained via potions.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:protean grace mutation"
msgid "Your limbs are overflowing with morphogenic power, guiding your every movement.\nEach other non-innate mutation you have also provides +1 EV and Slay +1 (up to\na limit of 7), improving your accuracy and damage with melee and ranged attacks.\nThis still applies even when the mutations would be suppressed by a different\nform.\n\nYou currently gain\n{{\n    num = math.min(you.how_mutated(true, false, false, true, false), 7) - 1\n    return \"+\" .. num .. \" EV and Slay +\" .. num .. \" from this mutation.\"\n}}\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:feed off suffering mutation"
msgid "A vile yet invigorating energy permeates your soul, which resonates with\nspecific other energies released at the moment of death. Whenever poisoned or\ndrained enemies die in sight of you, you have a chance to siphon away a small\nportion of their life force and magical power. Each level of this mutation\nincreases the chance this happens and the amount one siphons.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:lucky mutation"
msgid "You have an uncanny knack for uncovering hidden properties of items other people\nmight ignore as mundane. Whenever you encounter a new item on the ground for the\nfirst time, there is a very tiny chance to discover that it is actually an\nartefact. Each level of this mutation increases that chance.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:reckless mutation"
msgid "Your recklessness halves your SH, but the damage you deal with two-handed melee\nweapons is increased by 15%.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:horns mutation"
msgid "You have horns on your head, allowing you to try to gore foes when launching\nmelee attacks, but preventing you from wearing helmets. Each level of this mutation\nincreases the damage of your gore attacks. The third level of this mutation\nprevents you from wearing any type of headgear.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:beak mutation"
msgid "You have a beak for a mouth, allowing you to peck at foes when launching melee\nattacks, but preventing you from wearing helmets.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:fangs mutation"
msgid "You have sharp teeth, allowing you to bite foes when launching melee attacks.\nEach level of this mutation makes your teeth sharper, longer, and stronger.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:acidic bite mutation"
msgid "You have acidic saliva, allowing you to bite foes and apply corrosion to them\nwhen launching melee attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:antimagic bite mutation"
msgid "You have a thirst for magic, allowing you to bite foes when launching melee\nattacks. This restores your magical reserves and also disrupts magical foes,\nsometimes leaving them unable to act.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:claws mutation"
msgid "You have sharp nails, increasing the strength of your main-hand unarmed attacks\nif you aren't wearing gloves. If your off-hand is free, the strength of your\noff-hand punch attacks is also increased. Each level of this mutation makes your\nnails sharper, longer, and stronger. The third level of this mutation prevents\nyou from wearing gloves.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:demonic touch mutation"
msgid "Your hands glow with unholy energy, allowing you to inflict irresistible damage\nwhen launching melee attacks as long as your off-hand is free. Your touch\nattacks deal damage which ignores armour, and each level of this mutation\nincreases the damage dealt. The third level of this mutation prevents you from\nwearing gloves, and also causes your touch attacks to reduce your foes'\nwillpower.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hooves mutation"
msgid "You have hooves, allowing you to kick foes when launching melee attacks if you\naren't wearing boots. Each level of this mutation makes your hooves harder and\nstronger. The third level of this mutation prevents you from wearing boots.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:talons mutation"
msgid "You have sharp toenails, allowing you to kick foes when launching melee attacks\nif you aren't wearing boots. Each level of this mutation makes your nails\nsharper, longer, and stronger. The third level of this mutation prevents you\nfrom wearing boots.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:antennae mutation"
msgid "You have antennae, allowing you to sense nearby monsters, but preventing you\nfrom wearing helmets. Each level of this mutation lets you sense monsters from\nfurther away. The third level of this mutation prevents you from wearing any\ntype of headgear, and allows you to see invisible monsters.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:tentacle spike mutation"
msgid "You have a spiked tentacle, allowing you to pierce foes when launching melee\nattacks. Each level of this mutation makes your spike sharper, longer, and\nstronger.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:naga tail mutation"
msgid "You have a long, snake-like tail in place of feet. This gives you stability in\nwater, but causes you to move much more slowly than most species.\n\nThe second level of this mutation allows you to constrict foes with your tail\nwhen attacking in melee. Your tail can constrict only one enemy at a time, and\nevery attack at this mutation level will begin or renew a constriction attempt.\nThe chances of succeeding are determined primarily by Dexterity, Fighting skill,\nand any slaying bonuses.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:stinger mutation"
msgid "You have a venomous barb on the end of your tail, allowing you to sting foes\nwhen launching melee attacks. Each level of this mutation makes your stinger\nsharper, longer, and stronger.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:weakness stinger mutation"
msgid "You have a thin, orange tail, allowing you to attack foes when launching melee\nattacks. Each level of this mutation increases your tail's strength as it turns\ninto a sharp stinger. The third level of this mutation prevents wearing cloaks\nor scarves, and causes the stinger to inflict brief weakness on those struck.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:armoured tail mutation"
msgid "You have a large scaly tail, allowing you to tail-slap foes when launching\nmelee attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:pseudopods mutation"
msgid "You are covered in pseudopods, allowing you to slap your foes when launching\nmelee attacks, but somewhat reducing the amount of protection provided by body\narmour. This has no effect on protection provided by armour enchantments. Each\nlevel of this mutation makes your pseudopods larger and stronger.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:steam resistance mutation"
msgid "You are immune to the damage from steam breath and steam clouds.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mutation resistance mutation"
msgid "Your genetic code is unusually stable, making it less likely that you will be\nmutated (or have existing mutations removed). Each level of this mutation makes\nyou more resistant.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:torment resistance mutation"
msgid "You are less affected by unholy torment, which would otherwise cut your health\nin half. The second level of this mutation renders you entirely immune.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:poison resistance mutation"
msgid "You resist poisons, toxins and other contaminants. This does not confer\nfull immunity, but reduces your chances of being affected to roughly\none-third what it would be otherwise.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:fire resistance mutation"
msgid "You resist heat. Each level of this mutation reduces the amount of damage taken\nfrom fire.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:cold resistance mutation"
msgid "You resist cold. Each level of this mutation reduces the amount of damage taken\nfrom cold.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:electricity resistance mutation"
msgid "You resist electricity (rElec), taking less damage from it.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:acid resistance mutation"
msgid "You resist acid (rCorr), taking less damage from it. You are also less likely to\nbecome corroded.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:negative energy resistance mutation"
msgid "You resist negative energy. Each level of this mutation reduces the amount of\ndamage taken from negative energy, along with the amount that it drains your\nhealth.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:heat vulnerability mutation"
msgid "You are vulnerable to fire and take more damage from it.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:cold vulnerability mutation"
msgid "You are vulnerable to cold and take more damage from it.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:temperature sensitive mutation"
msgid "You are vulnerable to both cold and fire, and you take more damage from those\neffects.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:electricity vulnerability mutation"
msgid "You are vulnerable to electricity and take more damage from it.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:strong-willed mutation"
msgid "You have an unnaturally strong will, improving your resistance to certain types\nof hostile magic. Each level of this mutation increases your willpower further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:weak-willed mutation"
msgid "You have an unnaturally weak will, reducing your resistance to certain types\nof hostile magic. Each level of this mutation reduces your willpower further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:see invisible mutation"
msgid "You have supernaturally acute vision, allowing you to see monsters that are\nhidden from mundane sight. This has no effect on your accuracy otherwise.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:clarity mutation"
msgid "Your mind is exceptionally clear, preventing you from becoming confused,\nmesmerised, or supernaturally afraid. You also cannot become involuntarily\nenraged.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:sense surroundings mutation"
msgid "You have a second sense for the dungeon, allowing you to sense terrain you\nhaven't yet seen. Each level of this mutation allows you to sense more and\nmore thoroughly.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mp-powered wands mutation"
msgid "Your wands draw upon your magical reserves, becoming somewhat more powerful in\nthe process. If you have no magic left to draw upon, your wands function as\nnormal.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:regeneration mutation"
msgid "You heal quickly. Each level of this mutation increases the rate at which you\nheal.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:inhibited regeneration mutation"
msgid "You do not heal over time while foes are in sight. Other sources of healing,\nsuch as potions, are unaffected.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:speed mutation"
msgid "You cover ground quickly - moving to new positions takes you less time. This\nhas no effect on other types of actions, like attacking or casting spells.\nEach level of this mutation makes you move faster.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:slowness mutation"
msgid "You cover ground slowly - moving to new positions takes you more time. This\nhas no effect on other types of actions, like attacking or casting spells.\nEach level of this mutation makes you move slower.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:robust mutation"
msgid "You can take a beating. Each level of this mutation improves your health\nfurther (+10% HP).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:frail mutation"
msgid "Your health is poor. Each level of this mutation worsens your health further\n(-10% HP).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:evolution mutation"
msgid "As you gain experience, your body's hidden potential will express itself in the\nform of new beneficial mutations.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:devolution mutation"
msgid "As you gain experience, your body's hidden potential will express itself in the\nform of new detrimental mutations.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:wild magic mutation"
msgid "Your spells are slightly harder to cast (increased miscast chance and severity),\nbut more powerful. Each level of this mutation increases both effects further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:subdued magic mutation"
msgid "Your spells are slightly easier to cast (reduced miscast chance and severity),\nbut less powerful. Each level of this mutation increases both effects further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:teleportitis mutation"
msgid "You periodically teleport towards hostile monsters. You will sense yourself\ndestabilising several turns in advance of this happening, and may suppress it\ntemporarily by reading a scroll of teleportation.\n\nEach level of this mutation increases the frequency with which you start to\nteleport.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:deformed body mutation"
msgid "Your body is shaped in an inhuman way, somewhat reducing the amount of\nprotection provided by body armour. This has no effect on protection provided\nby armour enchantments.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:screaming mutation"
msgid "You are sometimes seized by an uncontrollable urge to scream whenever you\nsustain an injury. All of your shouts are also louder than usual.\n\nThe second level of this mutation makes this urge harder to resist and makes\nyour shouts even louder.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:poor constitution mutation"
msgid "Your body reacts poorly to injury; when you suffer damage, you have a chance for\nyour attacks to become weakened for a short while. The second level of this\nmutation sometimes additionally causes this to slow you as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no potion heal mutation"
msgid "Your system is resistant to potable panaceas, reducing the effectiveness of all\nkinds of healing potions. The second level of this mutation completely blocks\npotion-based healing. (Aid from the god Gozag is unaffected.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:persistent drain mutation"
msgid "Your health recovers twice as slowly from being drained. (The total experience\nneeded is doubled.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:contamination susceptible mutation"
msgid "You absorb twice as much mutagenic energy from being contaminated.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:initially attractive mutation"
msgid "The first time you see a monster, it may be magically pulled toward you. This\nhas a chance of waking sleeping monsters, though they will not necessarily be\nalerted to your position.\n\nThe second level of this mutation increases the chance of this happening.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:booming voice mutation"
msgid "When reading scrolls in sight of hostile monsters, your voice resounds with a\ntruly stupendous volume.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:potion fungus mutation"
msgid "Your body is covered in a fungus that metabolises beneficial potions you drink\nand sometimes emits spores which transmit their effects to nearby monsters -\nwhether friend or foe.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:spiteful blood mutation"
msgid "Your blood pulses with a strange predatory malice. When you become significantly\ninjured, some of it may rise up to attack you - though this may not happen again\nuntil you heal fully.\n\nThe second level of this mutation causes two erythrospites to be created at\nonce.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:spatial entanglement mutation"
msgid "Whenever you translocate, there is a chance that another monster is pulled\nalong to your new location. If you translocated a short distance, this will\nalways be some monster in sight of your previous location, but long distance\nteleports may pull a monster from anywhere else on the floor.\n\nThe experience is mildly disorienting for the monster, and it will not be able\nto act immediately after being pulled this way.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:meek mutation"
msgid "All of your attacks are less effective, dealing less damage and being a little\nless accurate. This effect worsens with each level of this mutation.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:demonic guardian mutation"
msgid "When you are harmed, there is a chance that a demonic guardian will rush to aid\nyou. The greater the damage done and the closer you are to death, the greater\nthe odds. Each level of this mutation increases the strength of your guardians.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:powered by pain mutation"
msgid "When you are harmed, you sometimes gain power, refilling your magical reservoir\nor granting you temporary agility or might. Each level of this mutation\nincreases the magnitude of this effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:foul stench mutation"
msgid "Foes who strike you in melee sometimes cause you to emit a cloud of foul\nmiasma, slowing and severely poisoning those caught within. This mutation also\ngrants you immunity to miasma (from any source).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:spiny mutation"
msgid "Foes who strike you in melee have a 50% chance of being impaled on your spines,\ntaking damage. Each level of this mutation increases the damage. This mutation\nalso grants you immunity to constriction.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:augmentation mutation"
msgid "While in good health, you are more powerful, casting spells and slaying foes\nmore effectively. Each level of this mutation increases this effect further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:magic shield mutation"
msgid "When you are harmed, some damage is redirected to deplete your magic reserves\ninstead.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:magic regeneration mutation"
msgid "Your magical reserves regenerate quickly.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:magic link mutation"
msgid "When low on magic, you restore magic in place of health.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:demonic magic mutation"
msgid "When you cast spells, there's a chance that adjacent monsters will be\nparalysed. This effect is more powerful when casting higher-level spells, but\nmonsters with greater willpower can resist it. Each level of this mutation\nallows more distant monsters to be affected. The third level of this mutation\nalso allows you to paralyse monsters in the same way while zapping wands.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:icemail mutation"
msgid "You are coated in a protective layer of ice. Fiery attacks can temporarily melt\nthe ice, removing the protection until the ice reforms. Each level of this\nmutation increases the protection imparted.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:condensation shield mutation"
msgid "A shield of frost guards you, blocking some incoming attacks. Fiery attacks can\ntemporarily melt the shield.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:powered by death mutation"
msgid "Death fuels you, allowing you to heal somewhat for each life you take. Each\nlevel of this mutation increases the healing further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:sturdy frame mutation"
msgid "Your movements are less encumbered by armour, mitigating penalties to spell\nsuccess, evasion, and ranged weapon attack speed. Each level of this mutation\nreduces armour ER by 2.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:sanguine armour mutation"
msgid "When seriously injured, your blood congeals around you, forming armour. Each\nlevel of this mutation increases the AC provided.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:big brain mutation"
msgid "What's up? You have a big brain. Each level of this mutation provides +2\nIntelligence. The third level of this mutation also makes you less likely to\nmiscast spells.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:ignite blood mutation"
msgid "Whenever you see blood spilled (yours or others'), it may ignite, bursting into\na cloud of flame. Each level of this mutation makes this more likely. This\nmutation also grants you immunity to clouds of flame.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:corrupting presence mutation"
msgid "When you injure a foe, your presence may intensify the damage, corroding their\nweapons and defences. The second level of this mutation makes matters even\nworse, adding a chance that your wretched victims may become mutated and\ndeformed.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:black mark mutation"
msgid "When you strike a foe in melee, the black mark on your body may debilitate\nthem. This can weaken foes, drain their health, or disrupt their magic.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:aura of silence mutation"
msgid "You are surrounded by an aura of silence. Those adjacent to you cannot make any\nsound. This prevents most forms of spellcasting and prayer, though demonic\nmagic, being noiseless, is unaffected.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:bedevilling mutation"
msgid "You cast Hex spells with increased power.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:demonic willpower mutation"
msgid "Your willpower has a will of its own, sometimes injuring those who attempt to\nhex you. This mutation also bolsters your willpower.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hurl damnation mutation"
msgid "You can hurl damnation with <input>$cmd[CMD_USE_ABILITY]</input>. Foes beware!\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:word of chaos mutation"
msgid "You can speak a Word of Chaos with <input>$cmd[CMD_USE_ABILITY]</input>.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:freezing cloud immunity mutation"
msgid "You are immune to freezing clouds.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:flame cloud immunity mutation"
msgid "You are immune to clouds of flame.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:spit poison mutation"
msgid "You can spit poison with <input>$cmd[CMD_USE_ABILITY]</input>. The second level\nof this mutation intensifies the poison from a single glob into a large cloud.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:in touch with death mutation"
msgid "Your Necromancy spells are more powerful. The second level of this mutation\nenhances your Necromancy spellpower further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:acrobatic mutation"
msgid "Your magical nature helps you tumble your hollow bones around acrobatically,\nallowing you to evade attacks while moving or waiting.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:evasive flight mutation"
msgid "You fly evasively through the air, increasing your evasion somewhat.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:big wings mutation"
msgid "You have big, strong wings, capable of bearing you through the air.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:float mutation"
msgid "You hover through the air, forever free from the surly bonds of earth.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:innate caster mutation"
msgid "You cannot learn spells from books. Instead, as you gain experience, spells\nof higher and higher level bubble up from your fiery heart. These spells are\nunpredictable and unaffected by your choice of god. You cannot train specific\nspell schools - you can either train all spell schools at once, or none.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hp casting mutation"
msgid "You have no separate reserve of magical power, and anything that would affect\nMP has no effect on you. When casting spells, using abilities, or doing anything\nelse that *costs* MP for normal species, you spend HP instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:extra vitality mutation"
msgid "You are built differently from most species, making you slightly harder to kill.\nEach level of this mutation increases your maximum health by 4 points.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:frog-like legs mutation"
msgid "Your uniquely muscular legs allow you to hop short distances in the blink of an\neye with <input>$cmd[CMD_USE_ABILITY]</input>, though your walking speed is\nbelow average.\n\nThe second level of this mutation allows you to hop farther.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:forlorn mutation"
msgid "You cannot, will not, and will never worship any god.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:nightstalker mutation"
msgid "You are attuned to the shadows, reducing the distance that you can see or be\nseen from.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:+los mutation"
msgid "The darkness flees at your approach, allowing an extended range of vision and\nranged attack (+1 line of sight), but also allowing you to be seen and\npotentially targeted from further away.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:stone body mutation"
msgid "You are made of living stone which is very resistant to damage. You gain a\nlarge bonus to your AC, which increases with your experience level, and are\nimmune to petrification, poison, disease, sleep, and certain other effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:cold-blooded mutation"
msgid "When struck by a cold attack, you may be slowed until your blood warms again.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:stealthy paws mutation"
msgid "When launching attacks on unaware or incapacitated enemies, you inflict extra\ndamage in the same way as a human with a short blade might.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:four strong arms mutation"
msgid "You have two pairs of two arms, allowing you to wield two-handed weapons with\none pair and a shield with the other, as well as wear a second pair of gloves.\nYou can even wield extremely large weapons like giant clubs, though this takes\nboth arm-pairs.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:retaliatory headbutt mutation"
msgid "When a foe misses you in melee, you may reflexively retaliate, attempting to\ngore your attacker with your horns. You can perform this against a given enemy\nno more than once per player action.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:multi-lived mutation"
msgid "Every third experience level, you gain an extra life. If you are slain while\nyou still have a life remaining, you will reappear elsewhere on the current\nfloor. You can continue to gain extra lives even once you reach the maximum\nexperience level, but you can't have more than two extra lives at a time.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:distributed training mutation"
msgid "Whenever you gain experience, it is split between all of your skills. You\ncannot choose to focus. Effects that prevent you from training certain skills,\nsuch as divine restrictions, do not cause your other skills to rise faster.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:nimble swimmer mutation"
msgid "You can blend into the waves while swimming through water or flying above it,\ngaining Stealth+. The second level of this mutation also grants you improved\nevasion and increases your movement speed when in or above water.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:divine attributes mutation"
msgid "Your divine heritage dramatically boosts your attribute gain (Strength,\nIntelligence, and Dexterity) as you level up relative to other species, both\nin quantity and in how much you can control your development. Every three\nlevels, you choose an attribute to increase by four points.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:tentacles mutation"
msgid "Your gelatinous body has eight tentacles that help you move around in shallow\nor deep water and can be used to constrict enemies while attacking them in\nmelee. Each tentacle can wear a ring, but you need four of them to move around,\nand either two or four tentacles to hold a weapon depending on its size, and\ntwo tentacles to hold a shield.\n\nYour tentacles will try to constrict foes which you attack in melee. (You cannot\nconstrict monsters larger than you.) Independently, you have a two-thirds chance\nof launching a damaging 'squeeze' attack whenever you attack in melee.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mertail mutation"
msgid "Your morphogenic matrix is unstable, allowing your lower body to shift to\na powerful aquatic tail when in shallow or deep water. It will quickly\nreform into legs once you leave the water. When in merform, your tail allows\nyou to tail-slap foes when launching melee attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no regeneration mutation"
msgid "You do not regenerate health while resting, and are unable to receive the\neffects of magic that hastens the speed of regeneration. Sources of HP gain\nsuch as potions and religious invocations will still heal you, and certain\ndivine invocations can temporarily grant you the ability to regenerate.\nTo compensate, you are able to heal yourself via the\n<input>$cmd[CMD_USE_ABILITY]</input> key, at the cost of permanent MP.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:treasure sense mutation"
msgid "You have an uncanny knack for knowing where items can be found. As you explore\nthe dungeon, nearby item locations that you cannot directly see will gain a\nmarker for as-yet-undiscovered items.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:gelatinous body mutation"
msgid "Your body is rubbery, and your foes like glue. Each level of this mutation\nconfers +1 AC and +1 EV.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:eyeballs mutation"
msgid "Golden eyeballs sprout from your flesh like mushrooms, confusing your foes.\nEach level of this mutation improves your accuracy with weapons, unarmed\nattacks, auxiliary attacks, and spells. The third level of this mutation also\nallows you to see invisible foes.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:translucent skin mutation"
msgid "Your skin is transparent, improving your stealth and reducing the accuracy of\nyour foes' attacks against you. Each level of this mutation reduces your foes'\naccuracy further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:slime shroud mutation"
msgid "When struck in melee, the slimy shroud covering your body may deflect the\nattack. Such deflection will break the shroud, however, and it will take time\nfor it to reconstitute itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:tendrils mutation"
msgid "When struck in melee, the slimy tendrils covering your body may grab your\nattacker's weapon and yank it from their grasp, disarming them.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:jelly sensing items mutation"
msgid "You have a small jelly attached to you that senses nearby items.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:jelly absorbing missiles mutation"
msgid "When struck by a missile (such as an arrow or crossbow bolt), a jelly on your\nbody may eat it, healing you somewhat.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:ooze flood mutation"
msgid "Your melee attacks may flood the airways of your foes with ooze. Those that need\nto breathe air will be silenced and take asphyxiation damage over time.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:otherworldly mutation"
msgid "You are foreign to this world, easily perceived by dark forces. You have only\none-tenth the normal time on each dungeon floor before Zot finds you and\npermanently reduces your health.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:foul shadow mutation"
msgid "Your body is shadowed by foul flame. Foes hit you less often, and your stealth\nis greatly enhanced. Foes who strike you in melee are sometimes seared by the\nfoul flame; undead and demonic foes take less damage from it, while holy beings\nand good god worshippers take more.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:explore regen mutation"
msgid "You are invigorated by the discovery of the unknown. As you explore the dungeon,\nyour wounds will heal and your magic will replenish.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:faith mutation"
msgid "You were embalmed with holy oils and sacred spices and bound about with blessed\ncloth. Now you have a special connection with the divine, allowing you to gain\nfavour with your chosen god more quickly.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:devour on kill mutation"
msgid "When you kill living creatures you have a chance to devour their life force\nand restore health.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:rollpage mutation"
msgid "You instinctively roll as you move toward enemies, rampaging forward an\nadditional space. This mutation grants magic regeneration when rolling; the\nsecond level of this mutation regenerates both magic and health.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:double potion healing mutation"
msgid "Whenever a potion restores your health or magic, it restores twice as much.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:drunken brawling mutation"
msgid "Whenever you drink a potion that restores health or magic, you perform an\nimmediate attack against all enemies adjacent to you.\n\n(This includes potions of curing, heal wounds, magic, and ambrosia.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:artefact enchanting mutation"
msgid "You can use scrolls of enchant weapon and armour upon random artefacts.\n\nThis cannot raise their enchantment above what would normally be possible on a\nmundane item of the same type, and cannot affect especially unusual artefacts\nat all.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:runic magic mutation"
msgid "You cast spells with the assistance of glyphs marked upon your armour, greatly\nreducing the complex movements which would otherwise be required.\n\n(The encumbrance rating of all armour you wear is reduced by 1/2 for purposes\nof spell success.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:off-hand wielding mutation"
msgid "You can wield a one-handed weapon in your off-hand, if not wielding a\ntwo-handed weapon or wearing a shield or orb. You will follow up attacks from\nyour primary weapon with your off-hand weapon, with attack delay determined\nby the average of the two. Ranged weapons can only follow attacks by ranged\nweapons, and likewise melee weapons.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:slow wielding mutation"
msgid "You must recalibrate your grasping arms when wielding or unwielding weapons.\nThis takes as long as donning armour.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:warmup strikes mutation"
msgid "Your grasping arms need time to limber up, making your first few attacks in\neach fight less effective. Slow, heavy attacks help more than fast attacks.\nBoth weapon-based and unarmed attacks are affected.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no rings mutation"
msgid "Your hands are embedded into the controls for your grasping arms, leaving you\nwith no place to wear magical rings without disastrous magical interference.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:iron-fused scales mutation"
msgid "Your scales are fused with a thick layer of iron, giving them resilience akin to\na suit of armour (+5 AC).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:inviolate magic mutation"
msgid "Your magic is resistant to distruption by outside forces. Hostile effects which\ndrain your MP drain only 1/3 as much and you are immune to having your magical\neffects be removed by anyone but yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:formless mutation"
msgid "You have no physical body and instead interact with the world through haunting\nobjects. This allows you to 'equip' up to 6 boots, gloves, cloaks, or helmets in\nany combination, but body armour is too large for you to usefully manipulate in\nthis fashion.\n\nAt higher levels, you can even send the armour you are haunting out to attack\nenemies independently for short periods of time.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:trickster mutation"
msgid "Your connection to the realm of the living grows stronger as you spread\nmisfortune.\n\nWhenever you or your allies inflict new negative status effects upon nearby\nenemies (other than poison), your AC temporarily increases. This bonus improves\nwith the number of different enemies affected, but will decay over time if you\ndo not continue to inflict new misfortune.\n\nAt your current XL, you can gain a maximum bonus of\n{{return 6 + you.xl() * 4 / 5;}} AC this way.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mnemnophage mutation"
msgid "You devour the memories and even the very existence of those you kill, subsuming\nthem in the unholy fire that wreathes your body.\n\nOnce you have accumulated enough memories, you may choose to burn them all away\nas fuel to greatly enhance your spellcasting for a short period of time.\n\nAt your current experience level, you can hold enough memories to cast a maximum\nof {{return 3 + math.floor(you.xl() * 3 / 20);}} spells in a row this way.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:spellclaws mutation"
msgid "You instinctually channel destructive magic through your claws. When casting a\ndamage-dealing spell, you also perform a melee attack against the most healthy\nenemy in your attack range.\n\nThis attack does reduced damage if your attack speed is slower than your casting\nspeed.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:accursed mutation"
msgid "Your accursed nature makes it harder to dispel malignant energies from your\nbeing, reducing the rate at which you can recover from Doom and Banes.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:vampire fangs mutation"
msgid "When you perform a stab attack against a living monster with blood, you heal for\na portion of the total damage dealt. When you are at 50% of your max HP or less,\nthis has a 2/3 chance to happen on all melee attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:bloodcurse mutation"
msgid "When you kill a living monster with a stab attack, they may rise again to serve\nyou as a vampiric thrall. Thralls possess all the abilities they had in life,\nbut are undead, and gain a vampiric bite attack as well as increased melee\ndamage against sleeping foes. Spellcaster thralls will also gain knowledge of\nthe Vampiric Draining spell.\n\nYou can only create a thrall if you do not have one already, and they will\ninevitably turn to dust after a short time.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:insubstantial mutation"
msgid "You are insubstantial and immune to many effects that require a solid body. You\ncannot be constricted, petrified, skewered by barbs, covered in sticky flame, or\ncaught by webs, nets, or bolas.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:frigid aura mutation"
msgid "Your breath radiates an intense chill which freezes nearby walls solid as you\nfight.\n\nWhenever you attack, you turn a random wall adjacent to your target or yourself\nfrigid. Frigid walls deal damage to any enemies adjacent to them each turn - up\nto twice as much when an enemy is adjacent to as many as possible. This damage\nincreases with your shapeshifting skill.\n\nThe walls will rapidly melt again if you stop attacking.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hive swarm mutation"
msgid "Whenever an injury reduces your health to less than 50%, angry bees will swarm\nout of your body to defend you.\n\nThis effect cannot happen again until your health is fully recovered.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:water reaching mutation"
msgid "Your body is formed of elemental water that changes shape flexibly, allowing you\nto reach and perform melee attacks from several tiles away - including auxiliary\nattacks of all sorts.\n\nThe precision required to preform stab attacks is still impossible unless you're\nadjacent to your target, and axes can only cleave to enemies adjacent to you,\nregardless of where you strike.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:water splatter mutation"
msgid "When injured, parts of your watery body may splatter as puddles of shallow\nwater nearby. The more damage you take at once, the more water is spread around.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:freeze response mutation"
msgid "Whenever you are exposed to cold damage, you have a chance to become Frozen,\nlowering your movement speed for a short while.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:steam response mutation"
msgid "Whenever you take fire damage from a projectile, you have a chance (increasing\nwith the damage suffered) to spread steam clouds around yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:airstrike attack mutation"
msgid "Whenever you strike an enemy in melee, the wind twists around and strikes them\nfor additional damage which is increased for each empty space adjacent to them.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hex mastery mutation"
msgid "Your Hexes spells are much easier to cast (reduced miscast chance and severity)\nand enemies have reduced willpower against all your effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:riddle compulsion mutation"
msgid "You compulsively pose riddles to enemies when you encounter them. Every now and\nagain, these may confuse or vex them, but mostly they just attract their\nattention.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:werefury mutation"
msgid "Whenever you kill an enemy with a melee attack, you gain a temporary slaying\nbonus that increases with your Shapeshifting skill.\n\nThis bonus stacks up to +12 and will not expire so long as you continue to\nperform melee attacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:werehowl mutation"
msgid "When you kill an enemy in melee, if your werefury bonus is at least 5, you may\nunleash a terrifying howl that attempts to freeze all nearby enemies in place\nwith fear if it overcomes their willpower.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:stinger hair mutation"
msgid "Your hair is made of long prehensile jellyfish-like tendrils. Whenever you\nattack in melee, they will lash out and attempt to poison several visible\nenemies within 2 tiles of the player at random.\n\nThe number of enemies they can afflict at once is improved by Shapeshifting\nskill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:lithotoxin mutation"
msgid "When your health drops to 60% of your max HP or less, your tendrils reflexively\nattempt to petrify all poisoned enemies within 3 tiles of you.\n\nThis effect cannot activate again until you fully heal and its success chance\nimproves with your Shapeshifting skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:contaminating mutation"
msgid "When you strike an enemy in melee, a portion of the transmutational energy\nsurging through will contaminate them. Upon landing the third strike in a row,\nthis contamination will grow so acute that it will twist their bodily form,\ninflicting significant AC-ignoring damage and malmutating them.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:maw attack mutation"
msgid "Your maw sometimes bites enemies in melee, at a rate independant of your own\nattack speed (once per 15 aut, on average). Its base damage is directly\nincreased by 75% of your strength.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:devouring maw mutation"
msgid "When you kill an edible enemy in melee (any hostile, non-summoned, living or\nplant monster), your maw devours it, greatly increasing your regeneration for a\nshort while. More powerful enemies provide a longer duration of this effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:growling stomach mutation"
msgid "Your maw's appetite is insatiable. Whenever you first encounter an edible\nmonster, there is a chance to become mesmerised by it and let out a loud growl.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:eel punch mutation"
msgid "Your melee hits have a 50% chance of discharging an arc of electricity that\ntravels between several connected enemies.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:eeljolt mutation"
msgid "When your health drops to 40% of your max HP, your eels violently discharge\nelectricity, striking all connected targets within 2 tiles of yourself - be they\nfriend or foe - for twice the damage their electricity normally does.\n\nThis effect cannot activate again until you fully heal.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:blade parry mutation"
msgid "You briefly gain SH equal to your slaying whenever you perform a melee attack\n(with an additional flat bonus based on your Shapeshifting skill).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:sporulate mutation"
msgid "Your melee attacks cause multiple burstshrooms to begin growing on unoccupied\ntiles behind your target. If they are allowed to grow for several turns, they\nwill explode in a burst of spores that damages nearby enemies and can briefly\ndaze them.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:armoured shell mutation"
msgid "Your body armour is fused into your shell, multiplying its base AC by a factor\nthat increases with your Shapeshifting skill. The effectiveness of its ego and\nmost artefact properties will also be doubled, though certain rare artefacts may\nbe too unusual to enhance this way.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hypnotic gaze mutation"
msgid "Your gaze has a hypnotic effect on many creatures. Whenever a living, demonic,\nor holy monster first notices you, it may become momentarily dazed, preventing\nit from shouting. Creatures which fail a Willpower check will be dazed for even\nlonger.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:gehenna destruction mutation"
msgid "Your Unleash Destruction ability is empowered by Gehenna. You are able to hurl\nbolts of magma instead of cold, and your bolts of fire can strip away their\nvictims' fire resistance.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:cocytus destruction mutation"
msgid "Your Unleash Destruction ability is empowered by Cocytus. You are able to hurl\nbolts of ice instead of fire, and your bolts of cold freeze victims solid,\nslowing their movement.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:tartarus destruction mutation"
msgid "Your Unleash Destruction ability is empowered by Tartarus. You are able to hurl\nbolts of devastation which lower their victims' willpower instead of lightning,\nand your bolts of negative energy are more powerful.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:dis destruction mutation"
msgid "Your Unleash Destruction ability is empowered by Dis. You are able to hurl bolts\nof corrosive acid and metal splinters instead of negative energy.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of the tyrant mutation"
msgid "Your Infernal Servants have a longer duration, and you can summon even stronger\nones at very high Invocations (at the cost of additional piety). When you slay\nenemies, a random servant may gain one of: Haste, Might, Regeneration, or\nBrilliance.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of carnage mutation"
msgid "Your Infernal Servants are now summoned adjacent to a random enemy instead of\nnear you. When they appear, they do so in a blast of your characteristic\nDestruction (which cannot hurt you or other allies).\n\n(Your servants unleash blasts of either\n{{\n    if you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Dis destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"acid or metal splinters\"\n    elseif you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Tartarus destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"negative energy or devastation\"\n    elseif you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Gehenna destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"fire or lava\"\n    elseif you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Cocytus destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"cold or ice\"\n    end\n}}.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of the legion mutation"
msgid "Your Infernal Servant ability is replaced with Infernal Legion, allowing you to\ncall in a small army of demons over time. While individually short-lived, their\nnumbers are inexhaustible for as long as the status lasts, and they will never\nbe hostile to you.\n\nYou may also unleash Makhleb's Destruction through your summoned allies without\nharming them.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of annihilation mutation"
msgid "Your Infernal Servant ability is replaced with Globe of Annihilation, allowing\nyou to hurl slow-moving spheres of intense destructive force that detonate\nin an enormous blast of your characteristic Destruction.\n\n(Your globes of annihilation unleash either\n{{\n    if you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Dis destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"acid or metal splinters\"\n    elseif you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Tartarus destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"negative energy or devastation\"\n    elseif you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Gehenna destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"fire or lava\"\n    elseif you.get_base_mutation_level(\"Cocytus destruction\") == 1 then\n        return \"cold or ice\"\n    end\n}}.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of haemoclasm mutation"
msgid "When you kill enemies, they have a small chance to explode in a violent rain of\ngore, which damages adjacent foes proportional to the victim's maximum health.\n\nEnemies who die to this explosion will always explode themselves.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of the celebrant mutation"
msgid "Whenever you fall below 50% of your maximum HP, you perform an infernal rite,\nunleashing a flurry of blood arrows aimed at nearby enemies. You will fire at\nleast one arrow for every valid target, but may fire additional ones if few are\npresent. Invocations increases the damage of these arrows, and Makhleb will\nalways bestow healing upon you for enemies killed by them.\n\nAfter performing a blood rite, you cannot perform another until your HP has\nfully recovered.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of execution mutation"
msgid "A demonic incarnation of murder lies dormant within your soul. Whenever you kill\nan enemy in melee, there is a small chance for it to awaken and manifest as a\nmaelstrom of scythe-like blades enveloping you. These blades will attack\nwhenever you do and also retaliate whenever you successfully dodge an enemy\nattack.\n\nYou have the ability to rampage swiftly towards enemies while possessed, but if\nyou spend more than a short while doing anything other than attacking, the\ndemonic presence will go dormant inside you once again.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of atrocity mutation"
msgid "Your Unleash Destruction is empowered the more times you use it consecutively\nwithout pause, increasing in both damage and health cost. Upon using it a fourth\ntime in a row, you will automatically unleash a wild barrage of additional bolts\naimed at random targets and reset this boost.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:mark of the fanatic mutation"
msgid "Grants you the Vessel of Slaughter ability, allowing you to transform into a\npowerful demonic avatar with greatly boosted offence and defence for a short\nperiod of time, in exchange for being dragged down into the Crucible of Flesh\nafterward and made to fight for your freedom.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:renounce potions mutation"
msgid "You refuse to drink potions in combat unless badly injured. While enemies are in\nyour line of sight (and for several turns afterward), you will be unable to\ndrink anything unless your HP is below 50% of its maximum.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:renounce scrolls mutation"
msgid "You refuse to read scrolls in combat unless badly injured. While enemies are in\nyour line of sight (and for several turns afterward), you will be unable to\nread anything unless your HP is below 50% of its maximum.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:missing a hand mutation"
msgid "You are considerably less handy than you used to be. You cannot wield\ntwo-handed weapons, wear a shield, launch off-handed attacks, or use more than\none magical ring at a time.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no stealth mutation"
msgid "You have no Stealth. You will always alert monsters when they see you, and\nthey will track you very effectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no forms mutation"
msgid "You cannot voluntarily change forms, via talismans, potions, or any other means.\nYou may still be involuntarily transformed by effects such as wands of polymorph.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:inability to use devices mutation"
msgid "You cannot use magical devices. This includes wands and most items evoked with\n<input>$cmd[CMD_EVOKE]</input>, excepting reaching weapons, talismans, and\nziggurat figurines. This also renders magical staves much less effective in\nmelee.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hated by all mutation"
msgid "You are hated by all and cannot acquire allies, temporarily or permanently, by\nany means. Simplistic, unthinking magical constructs, such as spectral weapons,\norbs of destruction, and battlespheres, are unaffected.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:cowardly mutation"
msgid "Seeing threatening monsters fills you with dread, reducing the power of your\nspells and your ability to slay monsters with melee and ranged attacks. Seeing\nmultiple such monsters at once, or extremely threatening monsters, renders this\neven worse.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:disrupted magic mutation"
msgid "Your chance to miscast spells is higher. Each level of this sacrifice increases\nthe chance to miscast further.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:unskilled mutation"
msgid "It takes you more experience to improve your skills. Each level of this\nsacrifice makes your skills cost even more.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:reduced ac mutation"
msgid "You are easily battered and bruised, reducing the benefits of your armour.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:reduced ev mutation"
msgid "Your reflexes are slow, making it easier for foes to hit you.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:inexperienced mutation"
msgid "You are inexperienced and unable to fully gain 27 levels.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:missing an eye mutation"
msgid "You are missing an eye, reducing the accuracy of your physical and magical\nattacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no potions mutation"
msgid "You are unable to quaff potions.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no weapons or thrown items mutation"
msgid "You are incapable of wielding weapons or throwing items.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no armour mutation"
msgid "You cannot wear armour.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:inability to train dodging mutation"
msgid "Your Dodging skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:inability to train armour mutation"
msgid "Your Armour skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no air magic mutation"
msgid "Your Air Magic skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no conjurations magic mutation"
msgid "Your Conjurations skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no earth magic mutation"
msgid "Your Earth Magic skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no fire magic mutation"
msgid "Your Fire Magic skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no hexes magic mutation"
msgid "Your Hexes skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no ice magic mutation"
msgid "Your Ice Magic skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no necromancy magic mutation"
msgid "Your Necromancy skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no alchemy magic mutation"
msgid "Your Alchemy skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no summoning magic mutation"
msgid "Your Summoning skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no translocations magic mutation"
msgid "Your Translocations skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:no forgecraft magic mutation"
msgid "Your Forgecraft skill is 0 and you cannot train the skill.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:lethargy bane"
msgid "You cover ground slowly - moving to new positions takes you 30% more time than\nnormal. This has no effect on other types of actions, like attacking or casting\nspells.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:heatstroke bane"
msgid "When you are damaged by fire, you have a high chance to become slowed for a\nmoderate length of time. This chance is decreased by your current level of fire\nresistance.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:snow-blindness bane"
msgid "When you are damaged by cold, you have a high chance to become weak and blind\nfor a moderate length of time. This chance is decreased by your current level of\ncold resistance.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:electrospasm bane"
msgid "When you are damaged by electricity, you have a chance to become bound in place\nand unable to move for a short length of time. This chance is decreased by your\ncurrent level of electrical resistance.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:claustrophobia bane"
msgid "You suffer a penalty to the damage and accuracy of your attacks and the power of\nyour spells based on the number of solid feature you are currently adjacent to.\n(-2 slaying and -10% spell power per solid feature.)\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:stumbling bane"
msgid "Your evasion is decreased by 25 on any turn where you either moved or waited in\nplace without doing anything else.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:succour bane"
msgid "Whenever you kill an enemy, all other nearby enemies are healed for a large\nfraction of that enemy's maximum health.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:multiplicity bane"
msgid "Enemies in sight of you have a small chance each turn to create multiple clones\nof themselves. These clones are very fragile and do reduced damage with attacks\nand spells, but will not expire on their own.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:dilettante bane"
msgid "Your skill with your highest weapon skill (at the time of becoming afflicted by\nthis bane) is reduced by 50% and your skill with your two highest skills among\nmagical schools, Invocations, and Evocations are reduced by 25%.\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:paradox bane"
msgid "Whenever you first spot a monster, it has a chance to become Touched by Paradox,\ngiving it the ability to use Manifold Assault as a natural ability (and a small\nchance to blink randomly after doing so).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:warding bane"
msgid "Groups of enemies you encounter may have Warding, preventing all damage they\nwould take from any source which is not currently adjacent to them (including\nclouds, if their creator is currently non-adjacent).\n"
msgstr ""

#: crawl-ref/source/dat/descript/mutations.txt
msgctxt "textdb:descript/mutations.txt:hunted bane"
msgid "Whenever an enemy summons monsters against you, they will usually appear\nadjacent to you instead of their summoner. Their duration is also increased and\nthey will never lose track of your position, no matter where you flee.\n"
